Object.assign(window.deepArticles, {
  "japanese-programming-languages": softwareArticle({
    slug:"japanese-programming-languages",title:"Japanese programming languages",eyebrow:"Japan and Co-Prosperity Sphere · 1947–1985",infoboxKicker:"Imperial software culture",
    lead:"Programming languages in Japan and the <a href='geacps.html'>Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere</a> developed around the computer as a controlled procedure. Scientific calculation, corporate administration, factory control, multilingual government, vertical text, embedded machinery, and a shortage of trained operators produced three principal native families: KEISAN, TEJUN, and Gakushū. Japan did not impose one instruction set or one corporate computer family. It standardized procedure descriptions, character identities, interchange media, peripheral commands, communications, and industrial interfaces across otherwise incompatible systems.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>",
    canon:"The relay, parametron, punched-card, procedure-sheet, and industrial-control origins; KEISAN, TEJUN, and Gakushū lineages; vertical native source; Imperial Kana Machine Code; sphere character registry; multinational runtime requirements; specialist research families; and 1980–85 UNICODE implementation are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Exact early code-point assignments, vendor dialect shares, individual compiler organizations, and complete grammars remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origins",title:"Procedural origins",html:"<p>Early Japanese computing combined relay calculators, parametron machines, telecommunications equipment, punched-card offices, analog controllers, and transistorized systems. Major corporations used cards and tabulators for accounts and inventories. Shipyards, mills, refineries, ports, telephone exchanges, and power systems developed written procedures for coordinating workers and machinery.</p><p>A standardized procedure sheet named each step, its inputs, the responsible clerk or machine, the expected output, an exception route, and any required authorization. Clerks, cards, relays, electronic equipment, or a mixed installation could execute the same administrative description. Early machine software itself remained numeric code, symbolic addresses, mathematical notation, and manufacturer conventions.</p>"},
      {id:"chronology",title:"Development, 1947–1985",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Principal developments</th><th>Characteristic use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1947–1955</td><td>Relay and parametron programming, numeric and symbolic orders, punched-card practice, procedure sheets, and dedicated industrial control.</td><td>Calculation, accounts, telecommunications, production, and logistics.</td></tr><tr><td>1955–1960</td><td>Transistor systems, the Imperial Kana Machine Code, KEISAN, TEJUN, common procedural specifications, and wider tape and printer use.</td><td>Science, administration, factories, ports, and government records.</td></tr><tr><td>1960–1965</td><td>Vertical native source, 計算二二 and 計算二四, 手順二三 and 手順二五, common character registry, multibyte interchange, corporate mainframes, and small process computers.</td><td>Engineering, online transactions, industrial supervision, and multilingual records.</td></tr><tr><td>1965–1970</td><td>計算二八, 手順二九, integrated circuits, factory networks, imperial transaction systems, graphics, and cross-compilation.</td><td>Aerospace, command systems, advanced industry, and linked production.</td></tr><tr><td>1970–1975</td><td>計算三二, 手順三三, microprocessors, distributed devices, database services, and stronger network control.</td><td>Instruments, transport, electronics, offices, and supervised automation.</td></tr><tr><td>1975–1980</td><td>計算三六, 手順三七, 学習三八, home computers, games, native word processing, and the Kyoto and Geneva universal-character work.</td><td>Professional systems, factory networks, schools, homes, and consumer electronics.</td></tr><tr><td>1980–1985</td><td>UNICODE 80, 学習四一, 計算四二, 手順四三, research languages, parallel packages, robots, and spacecraft-control profiles.</td><td>Multilingual networks, consumer systems, advanced automation, and space operations.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"institutions",title:"Institutional organization",html:"<p>The Imperial Army, Navy, ministries, universities, state laboratories, and corporate houses resisted surrendering their own computer systems. Fujitsu, NEC, Hitachi, Mitsubishi, and other manufacturers maintained different processors, operating environments, peripheral conventions, and compiler dialects. Central authorities therefore sought compatibility at the level of source specifications and exchange rather than requiring one national machine.</p><p>Standards covered tape labels, character registries, device classes, communications, procedure descriptions, and test programs. Ministries and large corporations maintained approved compiler lists and conversion centers. Universities and technical schools supplied programmers, while corporate training commonly tied language instruction to one installed system or industrial process.</p>"},
      {id:"sheets",title:"Procedure sheets and documentation",html:"<p>The procedure sheet remained part of Japanese software practice after high-level languages appeared. A production program was delivered with state diagrams, record layouts, device lists, authorization points, exception routes, restart instructions, and the names of responsible departments. Changes to a factory process required corresponding changes to software and operating documentation.</p><p>This practice was especially important where computers supervised older relays, mechanical equipment, or human work groups. A software fault could stop a rolling mill, misroute cargo, or leave a remote station waiting for authorization. Review therefore included the surrounding procedure as well as the compiler output.</p>"},
      {id:"representation",title:"Native source representation",html:"<p>Early source mixed Latin mathematical notation, short Latin or kana identifiers, katakana commands, Arabic numerals, and punched-card or paper-tape layouts. <a href='japanese-vertical-computing.html'>Vertical native source</a> became standard during the early 1960s. A program began in the upper-right corner of a portrait page. Characters descended within a statement, and successive statement columns proceeded to the left. Nested blocks appeared as inset vertical bands; diagnostic annotations occupied a neighboring column.</p><p>Horizontal left-to-right windows remained available for matrices, graphs, engineering drawings, imported algorithms, Latin-script material, and inexpensive printers. The stored source was a serialized stream of logical tokens and layout records rather than a two-dimensional image. A compiler read the same token sequence whether a terminal displayed a reserved word in katakana or an approved kanji form.</p>"},
      {id:"keisan",title:"KEISAN",html:"<p><a href='keisan.html'>KEISAN</a>, written ケイサン and later 計算, began around 1958–59 as the scientific language family. The common core provided floating-point arithmetic, formulas, arrays, loops, procedures, mathematical libraries, and formatted numerical output. Early corporate and university compilers were not interchangeable; the common standard defined portable source and required each implementation to document its limits.</p><p>計算二二 (<em>Keisan 22</em>) established native vertical source and Japanese positional numerals in 1962. 計算二四, 計算二八, and 計算三二 added blocks, separately compiled procedures, records, matrix and complex libraries, modules, scientific files, graphics, instruments, and cross-compilation. 計算三六 of 1976 improved professional modules and universal-character mappings. 計算四二 of 1982 supported sixteen- and thirty-two-bit systems, spacecraft, robotics, and parallel numerical libraries. Arrays began at one and used column-major storage.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"tejun",title:"TEJUN",html:"<p><a href='tejun.html'>TEJUN</a>, written テジュン and later 手順, descended from administrative and industrial procedure sheets. The first translators appeared around 1959–60 with records, exact decimal fields, tables, sorting, files, device operations, exceptions, states, and explicit human approval. It became more common than KEISAN in corporate administration and industrial installations.</p><p>手順二三 (<em>Tejun 23</em>) formalized records, files, state tables, devices, and approval in 1963. 手順二五 added events, timers, queues, restart, production batches, transaction confirmation, and durable process records. 手順二九, 手順三三, 手順三七, and 手順四三 supplied cooperating procedures, rollback, maintenance states, event priorities, acknowledged messages, distributed calls, reservations, lost-link behavior, and explicit safe states. Embedded profiles fixed memory and queue bounds and prohibited recursion and dynamic allocation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"hardware",title:"Hardware and operating environments",html:"<p>Japanese manufacturers built transistorized mainframes with magnetic-core memory, drums, tape units, kana terminals, Japanese printers, and corporate operating systems. Parametron systems remained in universities, naval establishments, and scientific centers into the early 1960s. They lost most new commercial orders after transistor equipment became more reliable and economical.</p><p>Small twelve-, eighteen-, and twenty-four-bit process computers entered machine tools, rolling mills, refineries, cranes, shipyards, telephone systems, and electrical dispatch. These systems were commonly sold as components of a complete industrial installation. Their software was maintained by the equipment maker, the corporate engineering office, or a resident team rather than circulated as an independent mass-market product.</p>"},
      {id:"kana-code",title:"Imperial Kana Machine Code",html:"<p>The late-1950s <a href='imperial-kana-machine-code.html'>Imperial Kana Machine Code</a> supplied a compact seven-bit core containing katakana, the numerals 〇一二三四五六七八九, punctuation, arithmetic signs, controls, and vertical-layout instructions. Reduced subsets remained in calculators, controllers, printers, and communications devices. More capable systems used shifts or supplementary tables for Latin text and additional symbols.</p><p>Kanji initially required numeric dictionary references, special printers, photographic or mechanical output, and paper or microfilm cross-reference. Tokenized programming terminals offered an economical intermediate step: a typed katakana command could display as a kanji keyword while the stored program retained one compact code.</p>"},
      {id:"registry",title:"Co-Prosperity Sphere character registry",html:"<p>Tokyo established the <a href='co-prosperity-sphere-common-character-code.html'>Co-Prosperity Sphere character registry</a> around 1962–63. Each registered character received a script class, number, preferred form, permitted variants, sorting class, vertical and horizontal rendering properties, and mappings to manufacturer codes. The registry expanded from Japanese and a Philippine Latin supplement to Hangul, traditional Chinese, Thai, Burmese, Vietnamese, Malay and Indonesian orthographies, mathematical signs, and commercial symbols.</p><p>A multibyte interchange form followed in 1964–65. Common Japanese characters retained compact forms, while a leading unit selected larger script tables. Older systems used translation tables and high-end terminals used font memory to convert abstract identities into visible glyphs. Duplicate ideographs, compatibility characters, and corporate additions remained in use.</p>"},
      {id:"philippines",title:"The Philippine programming community",html:"<p><a href='philippines.html'>The Philippines</a> developed one of the sphere's largest non-Japanese programming communities. Schools in Manila taught Japanese technical vocabulary, vertical source, TEJUN procedure analysis, Latin supplementary libraries, and conversion between imperial and Filipino records. Filipino programmers worked in banking, shipping, English-language commercial systems, communications, mixed-script databases, and conversion to American formats.</p><p>Latin script was common in names, comments, local records, output, and selected identifiers, while keywords and many programming numerals remained Japanese. Advanced appointments in the central establishment usually required high Japanese literacy. The community therefore supplied important multilingual expertise under unequal institutional access.</p>"},
      {id:"sphere",title:"Regional use in the Co-Prosperity Sphere",html:"<p><a href='china.html'>China</a>, <a href='manchukuo.html'>Manchoukuo</a>, Thailand, Burma, Indochinese administrations, and other sphere territories used Japanese compilers, procedure standards, and interchange services in different proportions. Local-language output supported censuses, customs, trade, transport, taxation, and industrial records. Japanese commonly remained the language of compiler documentation, central technical education, and interregional administration.</p><p>Corporate and government installations could exchange registered text and documented records while retaining incompatible processors. Local centers adapted forms, dictionaries, and reports, but Tokyo and the principal Japanese companies controlled much advanced processor design, compiler maintenance, network infrastructure, and specialist libraries.</p>"},
      {id:"applications",title:"Industrial and administrative applications",html:"<p>TEJUN systems supervised warehouses, ports, refineries, telephone exchanges, production schedules, railway terminals, trading-house accounts, carrier maintenance, and electric-power dispatch. KEISAN supported ship design, structural analysis, navigation, meteorology, materials work, and industrial research. Corporate installations often joined a central mainframe to smaller controllers and relay equipment.</p><p>Persistent records and explicit exception routes suited operations spread across long distances. A process could wait for an acknowledgment, request supervisory approval, enter a maintenance state, or restart from a checkpoint. Routine offices encountered the system through forms and reports even when the underlying computers remained inside factories, exchanges, logistics centers, and large corporate compounds.</p>"},
      {id:"military",title:"Military and space systems",html:"<p>Japanese software supported air-defense plotting, missile trials, naval logistics, carrier maintenance, reconnaissance processing, communications, and production control. KEISAN cross-compilers produced numerical and guidance software for smaller targets. Embedded TEJUN represented devices and missions as bounded processes with named events, deadlines, authorities, and failure states.</p><p>Later TEJUN profiles handled spacecraft and robots through acknowledged messages, reservations, timeouts, lost-link behavior, and supervised autonomy. A vehicle could enter a declared safe state after loss of communications rather than continue an unbounded procedure. KEISAN supplied trajectory, structural, sensor, and scientific computation alongside this operational layer.</p>"},
      {id:"gakushu",title:"Gakushū and consumer programming",html:"<p><a href='gakushu.html'>Gakushū</a> entered schools and homes as 学習三八 in 1978. Its interpreter offered immediate commands, arithmetic, conditions, loops, arrays, vertical text, graphics, sound, controllers, cassette storage, and cartridge access. Japanese numerals and tokenized native commands allowed a learner to write a program without knowing English keywords or Latin letters.</p><p>学習四一 of 1981 added UNICODE identity, horizontal and vertical windows, improved sprites and sound, disks, communications, structured procedures, and manufacturer compatibility profiles. Firms shared an educational core while extending graphics, music, controllers, and storage. Magazines, schools, and clubs identified the machine profile required by a published listing.</p>"},
      {id:"consumer",title:"Text and consumer electronics",html:"<p>Native programming encouraged early investment in font ROMs, bitmap glyphs, character printers, portrait displays, high-resolution cathode-ray terminals, dictionary input, sorting, normalization, and electronic typesetting. Keyword conversion expanded gradually into kanji identifiers, technical dictionaries, word processors, publishing, and electronic mail.</p><p>The same capabilities entered home computers, game consoles, arcade machines, electronic dictionaries, office terminals, navigation equipment, and industrial displays. Factory experience supplied inexpensive integrated circuits and display components; consumer production in turn lowered the cost of terminals and embedded control.</p>"},
      {id:"research",title:"Research languages",html:"<p>関数四二 (Kansū 42) applied ML and American Lambda ideas through native tokens. 論理四四 (Ronri 44) applied the Marseille Prolog search model to UNICODE atoms. 画像四四 (Gazō 44) used retained scene objects and event procedures for graphics and interface research.</p><p>These systems appeared in universities, corporate laboratories, graphics work, language processing, proof research, and experimental knowledge systems. They remained specialist languages in 1985. Their compatibility profiles were more stable than their institutional names and complete standard libraries.</p>"},
      {id:"foreign",title:"Foreign languages and translation",html:"<p>Japanese universities and corporations also used FORTRAN, COBOL, C-like systems tools, Pascal, Ada, Prolog, and other imported languages. Foreign source usually ran through a horizontal editor, an import character profile, and a documented runtime mapping. Programs that assumed an eight-bit byte, American character order, or Latin filenames required explicit conversion.</p><p>KEISAN and TEJUN source moved among corporate machines through common specifications, test suites, and cross-compilers. This was source portability rather than binary compatibility. Data conversion remained a separate task because record encodings, collations, numeric formats, and peripheral behavior differed.</p>"},
      {id:"unicode",title:"UNICODE 80",html:"<p>Japanese experience with multilingual registry work shaped the 1977 Kyoto draft and the 1978–79 Geneva compromise that produced <a href='unicode-80.html'>UNICODE 80</a>. The 1980 Tokyo–Melbourne congress established sixteen-bit abstract character identities while allowing compact local encodings. Japanese and sphere standards bodies adopted the system more consistently than the American market.</p><p>Post-1980 runtimes exposed operations to decode local bytes, encode a registered identity when possible, render it according to vertical or horizontal properties, and compare it under a named collation. Binary equality referred to registered identity rather than visual similarity. Traditional Chinese remained the standard Chinese form.</p>"},
      {id:"position",title:"Position in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Japan led in native non-Latin programming, multilingual character architecture, vertical terminals, industrial process languages, compact consumer display, and supervised automation. KEISAN served science and engineering, TEJUN served durable administration and control, and Gakushū supplied a large school and household programming public. Imported and research languages continued beside them.</p><p>The sphere did not possess one compatible computer market. Corporate processors, operating environments, and consumer machines remained diverse. Common source specifications, token identity, character registries, interfaces, and conversion practice allowed those systems to participate in an imperial information order.</p>"}
    ],related:[...japaneseSoftwareRelated,{href:"japan.html",kicker:"Imperial center",label:"Empire of Japan"},{href:"geacps.html",kicker:"Political and economic sphere",label:"Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere"},{href:"unicode-80.html",kicker:"Universal character standard",label:"UNICODE 80"},{href:"philippines.html",kicker:"Multilingual programming center",label:"Philippines"},{href:"microelectronic-household.html",kicker:"Consumer context",label:"Microelectronic Household"},{href:"software-portability.html",kicker:"Cross-machine practice",label:"Software Portability"},{href:"programming-languages.html",kicker:"Global overview",label:"Programming Languages"}],facts:[["Governing conception","Computer as controlled procedure"],["Principal institutions","Ministries, services, universities, zaibatsu, standards bodies"],["Scientific family","KEISAN · from 1958–59"],["Administrative/control family","TEJUN · from 1959–60"],["Home/educational family","Gakushū · from 1978"],["Canonical source layout","Vertical token stream · top-to-bottom, columns right-to-left"],["Early character core","Imperial Kana Machine Code · late 1950s"],["Sphere registry","From 1962–63"],["Shared identity standard","UNICODE 80"],["1985 research families","Kansū, Ronri, Gazō"]]
  }),

  "imperial-kana-machine-code": softwareArticle({
    slug:"imperial-kana-machine-code",title:"Imperial Kana Machine Code",eyebrow:"Japanese character encoding · late 1950s",infoboxKicker:"National machine-character standard",
    lead:"The Imperial Kana Machine Code was Japan's first common machine-character standard. Adopted in the late 1950s, it defined a compact seven-bit core for katakana, Japanese numerals, punctuation, arithmetic notation, device control, and vertical page layout. It allowed telecommunications equipment, punched-tape systems, computers, printers, and industrial controllers to exchange ordinary Japanese technical text while individual manufacturers retained different processors and internal formats.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>",
    canon:"The late-1950s origin, seven-bit Japanese core, katakana and native numeral repertoire, arithmetic and control functions, vertical-layout support, reduced device subsets, Latin supplement, later multibyte registry, and eventual UNICODE 80 mapping are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Exact bit assignments, escape values, mandatory control positions, and the full list of early vendor variants remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"background",title:"Background",html:"<p>Japanese computing after 1947 grew among telecommunications offices, punched-card departments, universities, naval and industrial laboratories, relay installations, parametron machines, and early transistor computers. Each manufacturer could assign different numeric values to kana, punctuation, printer actions, and tape controls. A record prepared for one machine often required transcription or a translation table before another could read it.</p><p>The emerging KEISAN and TEJUN language families increased demand for common source media. Factory and administrative systems also required compact codes for names, labels, quantities, machine states, and operator messages. Full kanji representation remained too expensive for routine memory, tape, and terminal equipment.</p>"},
      {id:"standardization",title:"Standardization, 1957–1959",html:"<p>Government laboratories, telecommunications authorities, equipment makers, universities, and large corporate users prepared a common kana code during the electronics and automation expansion of 1957–59. The standard served as a shared interchange floor rather than a compulsory processor character set. A manufacturer could use a different internal code if its tape, terminal, and communications equipment supplied a documented mapping.</p><p>The national standard fixed a seven-bit core. Specialized devices were permitted to implement reduced repertoires, and larger systems could select supplementary tables. The published code was accompanied by printing, tape, and layout rules rather than only a character chart.</p>"},
      {id:"objectives",title:"Design objectives",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Implementation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Compact storage</td><td>A seven-bit core suitable for tape, telecommunications, small memories, and controllers.</td></tr><tr><td>Native technical text</td><td>Katakana, Japanese positional numerals, punctuation, and common arithmetic signs.</td></tr><tr><td>Page layout</td><td>Controls for vertical writing and movement between statement columns.</td></tr><tr><td>Device operation</td><td>Nonprinting controls for readers, punches, printers, terminals, and communications.</td></tr><tr><td>Legacy equipment</td><td>Reduced subsets and explicit translation tables.</td></tr><tr><td>Expansion</td><td>Shift conventions and supplementary repertoires for Latin text and later registries.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"repertoire",title:"Core repertoire",html:"<p>The core contained katakana, Japanese punctuation, the digits 〇一二三四五六七八九, arithmetic signs including 加, 減, 乗, 除, 負, and 等, spacing and separation marks, and device-control functions. Programming and scientific profiles could associate common kata­kana sequences with language tokens. The code did not attempt to encode the general kanji writing system.</p><p>Exact code positions differed in preliminary and manufacturer systems, and the final bit-assignment table has not been fixed in the surviving general references. Publications therefore identify characters and functions by registered name when a numeric value is not material.</p>"},
      {id:"storage",title:"Storage and transmission",html:"<p>Seven-bit units could be stored directly in machines designed around the national code or packed into larger words. Tape and communications profiles declared framing, parity, shift state, record boundaries, and the treatment of damaged units. A file transferred between unlike systems was decoded into named characters and controls before being re-encoded for the receiving device.</p><p>Small controllers sometimes reserved only the subset required by their panel, printer, or process vocabulary. A reduced implementation documented unsupported characters and the replacement mark it produced. Industrial protocols distinguished display characters from device commands so that corrupted text would not ordinarily actuate machinery.</p>"},
      {id:"vertical",title:"Vertical layout",html:"<p>The code included controls needed for Japanese vertical composition. Characters within a line descended from top to bottom. A following statement or text column normally began to the left of the preceding one. Portrait terminals, printers, and program listings used column boundaries, indentation metadata, and page controls to reproduce this order.</p><p>Horizontal left-to-right modes remained available for mathematical formulas, matrices, graphs, engineering drawings, Latin-script text, and equipment unable to buffer a vertical page. Layout controls described presentation; compilers and record-processing programs consumed a sequential character stream.</p>"},
      {id:"programming",title:"Programming-language use",html:"<p>Early KEISAN source combined mathematical notation, short identifiers, katakana commands, and either Arabic or Japanese numerals. KEISAN 62 made a fully native vertical representation normal. TEJUN used the code for record names, states, devices, exception routes, and authorization points. Common words included モシ, ソノタ, クリカエシ, ヨミコミ, カキダシ, and オワリ.</p><p>Compilers tokenized reserved words where memory was scarce. A source file could therefore contain a compact language token rather than each displayed character. Older printers rendered its katakana form; later terminals could render an approved kanji form without changing program identity.</p>"},
      {id:"input",title:"Input equipment",html:"<p>Paper-tape punches, card equipment, telecommunications keyboards, and early terminals supplied katakana and numeral input. Specialized programming keyboards grouped common control words and editing functions. Scientific installations retained keys or shift states for Latin formulas and imported notation.</p><p>General kanji input remained a separate procedure. Operators entered dictionary numbers, selected characters from indexed lists, or sent text to specialist composition equipment. Limited keyword conversion was practical much earlier because a compiler recognized only a few dozen reserved terms.</p>"},
      {id:"output",title:"Printing and kanji output",html:"<p>Kana printers and teleprinters supplied routine listings, messages, labels, and reports. Full names and legal text could be retained as numeric dictionary references or as paper and microfilm images beside the machine record. High-value offices used photographic, mechanical, or specialized character printers for kanji output.</p><p>Terminal font memories later mapped token and registry identities to bitmap glyphs. A device with a limited font substituted katakana, a registered simplified display, or a visible missing-character mark. The stored identity and visible form were kept conceptually separate.</p>"},
      {id:"latin",title:"Latin supplement",html:"<p>The <a href='philippines.html'>Philippines</a> and international commercial traffic required Latin capitals and lowercase, Filipino and English punctuation, diacritics, horizontal text controls, and optional Latin identifiers. These entered through a supplementary repertoire rather than displacing the compact Japanese core.</p><p>Filipino programmers commonly used Japanese language tokens and numerals while writing names, comments, local records, reports, and selected identifiers in Latin script. Shipping, banking, communications, and American-format conversion made mixed-script operation an ordinary production requirement.</p>"},
      {id:"devices",title:"Device profiles",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Equipment</th><th>Typical repertoire</th><th>Principal constraints</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Calculator or panel controller</td><td>Digits, arithmetic, status kana, and device controls.</td><td>Very small memory and fixed display.</td></tr><tr><td>Teleprinter</td><td>Katakana, numerals, punctuation, spacing, and transmission controls.</td><td>Line speed, shift state, and limited printing mechanism.</td></tr><tr><td>Programming terminal</td><td>Core code, language tokens, editing, and vertical-layout controls.</td><td>Portrait display memory and compiler profile.</td></tr><tr><td>Corporate mainframe terminal</td><td>Core plus Latin supplement and registry mappings.</td><td>Vendor operating system and font library.</td></tr><tr><td>Industrial computer</td><td>Process vocabulary, alarms, records, and isolated device commands.</td><td>Deterministic operation and safe failure.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"administration",title:"Administrative and industrial use",html:"<p>The standard appeared in government records, trading-house accounts, warehouse labels, shipping manifests, production schedules, port systems, telephone exchanges, railway terminals, and power dispatch. Common character names simplified conversion among corporate computers even when the record structures and instruction sets differed.</p><p>Industrial messages were deliberately short. Operators received kana status reports, numbered exceptions, confirmation requests, and named machine states. TEJUN documentation connected those messages to procedure sheets, responsible offices, restart points, and authorization rules.</p>"},
      {id:"registry",title:"Sphere character registry",html:"<p>The seven-bit core could not represent the languages administered across the <a href='geacps.html'>Co-Prosperity Sphere</a>. Around 1962–63, Tokyo established the broader <a href='co-prosperity-sphere-common-character-code.html'>Co-Prosperity Sphere Common Character Code</a>, giving each entry a script class, registry number, preferred form, variants, sorting class, layout properties, and mappings to manufacturer codes.</p><p>A multibyte interchange form followed in 1964–65. Japanese core characters retained compact representations, while leading units selected kanji, Latin, Hangul, traditional Chinese, Thai, Burmese, Vietnamese, Malay and Indonesian, mathematical, and technical tables. Imperial Kana Machine Code remained the common Japanese local form beneath the registry.</p>"},
      {id:"compatibility",title:"Compatibility and conversion",html:"<p>Conformance did not require identical bytes inside every machine. A device or compiler declared its implemented subset, shift and framing rules, replacement behavior, vertical-layout support, and mappings to the common names. Exchange tests compared decoded character identity and required control behavior.</p><p>Collation remained separately named. Katakana order, numeric order, Latin order, and local administrative sorting could produce different sequences. Visual similarity did not make two encoded forms identical, and conversion programs recorded substitutions or unrepresentable characters.</p>"},
      {id:"consumer",title:"Influence on consumer electronics",html:"<p>Support for compact Japanese text encouraged investment in font read-only memories, bitmap generators, portrait displays, character printers, dictionary lookup, and tokenized editors. These components later entered word processors, home computers, game consoles, arcade machines, electronic dictionaries, navigation displays, and office terminals.</p><p>The 1978 Gakushū language used tokenized vertical source on inexpensive consumer systems. Its interpreters could retain the familiar kana core while cartridges, disks, and more capable machines added expanded graphics, sound, communications, and larger character mappings.</p>"},
      {id:"unicode",title:"Mapping to UNICODE 80",html:"<p>The code's distinction between local representation and registered character identity influenced the Japanese universal-character program. The 1977 Kyoto draft and later international negotiations produced <a href='unicode-80.html'>UNICODE 80</a>, which assigned sixteen-bit abstract identities while permitting compact national and manufacturer encodings.</p><p>Japanese runtimes after 1980 decoded local units to UNICODE identity, encoded an identity when the target repertoire allowed it, rendered vertical or horizontal presentation, and compared text under a named collation. Existing kana files and embedded devices did not require immediate replacement.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy",html:"<p>Imperial Kana Machine Code remained common in controllers, telecommunications equipment, archived tapes, programming tools, and inexpensive devices after multibyte standards appeared. Gateways and operating systems maintained conversion tables for older files.</p><p>Its principal successor systems expanded character coverage rather than abolishing the compact core. By 1985 the code was simultaneously a legacy interchange format, an embedded-device repertoire, and the historical base of Japan's native programming and multilingual standards practice.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"japanese-programming-languages.html",kicker:"Software context",label:"Japanese Programming Languages"},{href:"keisan.html",kicker:"Scientific language",label:"KEISAN"},{href:"tejun.html",kicker:"Administrative and control language",label:"TEJUN"},{href:"gakushu.html",kicker:"Consumer language",label:"Gakushū"},{href:"unicode-80.html",kicker:"Universal successor",label:"UNICODE 80"},{href:"philippines.html",kicker:"Latin supplement",label:"Philippines"},{href:"geacps.html",kicker:"Regional setting",label:"Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere"},{href:"computing.html",kicker:"Technology overview",label:"Computing"}],facts:[["Type","Seven-bit machine-character standard"],["Introduced","Late 1950s"],["Principal repertoire","Katakana, Japanese numerals, punctuation, arithmetic signs"],["Presentation","Vertical and horizontal controls"],["Primary media","Paper tape, communications, terminals, printers, controllers"],["Expansion","Shifted and supplementary repertoires"],["Sphere registry","From 1962–63"],["Multibyte interchange","From 1964–65"],["Universal mapping","UNICODE 80"],["Exact bit assignments","Open"]]
  }),

  "co-prosperity-sphere-common-character-code": softwareArticle({
    slug:"co-prosperity-sphere-common-character-code",title:"Co-Prosperity Sphere Common Character Code",eyebrow:"Multilingual character registry · from 1962–63",infoboxKicker:"Imperial character and interchange standard",
    lead:"The Co-Prosperity Sphere Common Character Code (Japanese working title: 共栄圏共通文字符号) was the multilingual character registry and interchange system developed in Tokyo from 1962–63. It assigned stable identities and presentation information to characters used in Japanese and associated-state administration, then supplied a multibyte interchange form by 1964–65. The standard allowed banks, ports, ministries, corporations, and communications systems to exchange names and records across incompatible Japanese computers and the principal scripts of the <a href='geacps.html'>Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere</a>.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>",
    canon:"The 1962–63 registry, 1964–65 multibyte form, Japanese compact-core continuity, script scope, registry fields, vendor mappings, Philippine catalyst, 1970 practical maturity, compatibility defects, and influence on UNICODE 80 are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The Japanese and English titles remain working conventional names rather than a confirmed final statutory title. Exact code assignments, block numbers, issuing offices, and national implementation dates remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"background",title:"Background",html:"<p><a href='imperial-kana-machine-code.html'>Imperial Kana Machine Code</a> provided a compact Japanese core during the late 1950s. Corporate systems could exchange katakana, numerals, punctuation, arithmetic signs, and device controls through documented mappings. Kanji and non-Japanese scripts still depended on dictionary numbers, special equipment, paper records, or manufacturer code pages.</p><p>The postwar sphere contained Chinese, Korean, Philippine, Thai, Burmese, Vietnamese, Malay and Indonesian writing communities. Shipping, customs, finance, taxation, population records, education, and industrial administration required names and text to move between jurisdictions.</p>"},
      {id:"philippines",title:"The Philippine catalyst",html:"<p>The <a href='philippines.html'>Philippines</a> entered the Japanese-associated order with a large Latin-script bureaucracy, English-language higher education, Latin-written Filipino languages, and commercial records using Latin personal and corporate names. Its associated-state position gave Manila greater leverage over technical standards than administrations previously expected to accept Japanese numeric codes and paper cross-reference.</p><p>A Latin supplementary package supplied capitals and lowercase, Filipino and English punctuation, common diacritics, horizontal text controls, printers, translation tables, record libraries, and optional Latin identifiers. Its adoption immediately raised the wider problem of registering the remaining scripts used throughout the sphere.</p>"},
      {id:"formation",title:"Formation, 1962–1963",html:"<p>Tokyo created a central registry around 1962–63. The register described characters independently of one manufacturer's processor, font, or storage code. Vendors submitted mappings from their installed encodings and registered extensions used by existing customers.</p><p>The standard began as an identity catalogue and conversion reference. It did not initially prescribe one byte sequence for every character. Government and corporate exchanges could name a registry entry and select an agreed transmission profile while retaining local formats inside each installation.</p>"},
      {id:"record",title:"Registry record",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Script classification</td><td>Identified the writing system and applicable technical rules.</td></tr><tr><td>Registry number</td><td>Supplied a stable machine-independent identity.</td></tr><tr><td>Preferred form</td><td>Recorded the authorized reference shape.</td></tr><tr><td>Permitted variants</td><td>Connected accepted national, historical, and typographic forms.</td></tr><tr><td>Sorting class</td><td>Supported named administrative and linguistic collations.</td></tr><tr><td>Presentation properties</td><td>Described vertical and horizontal behavior.</td></tr><tr><td>Vendor mappings</td><td>Related installed codes to the common identity.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"scope",title:"Script coverage",html:"<p>The register progressively covered Japanese kana and kanji, the Philippine Latin supplement, Hangul, traditional Chinese, Thai, Burmese, Vietnamese, Malay and Indonesian spelling, mathematical notation, commercial marks, and technical symbols. Indigenous and minority names could be registered where administrative demand and available expertise justified the work.</p><p>Traditional Chinese forms supplied the general Chinese standard. No Communist Chinese government conducted a state simplification program in the setting. Japanese kanji variants and Chinese forms could share a registry relationship while retaining different preferred national presentation.</p>"},
      {id:"multibyte",title:"Multibyte interchange, 1964–1965",html:"<p>A common multibyte interchange form appeared by 1964–65. Frequent Japanese core characters retained compact units. A leading code selected supplementary tables, and kanji or regional-script entries used two or more units. Record headers identified the profile, revision, and any private extension used.</p><p>The design favored conversion and transmission over uniform internal storage. Mainframes could translate the interchange stream into corporate codes. Small terminals stored only selected fonts, while archival systems could preserve identities whose glyphs they could not display.</p>"},
      {id:"kana",title:"Japanese compact-core continuity",html:"<p>The sphere system did not abolish the seven-bit Japanese code. Telecommunications equipment, industrial controllers, calculators, and programming tools continued to use compact kana. Gateways mapped these local units into the common registry.</p><p>Japanese text therefore occupied a privileged technical position. It was cheaper to store, easier to display on small devices, and more consistently supported than many associated-state scripts. Full multilingual conformance required additional tables, font memory, and conversion software.</p>"},
      {id:"mapping",title:"Manufacturer mappings",html:"<p>Fujitsu, NEC, Hitachi, Mitsubishi, state laboratories, and smaller manufacturers retained different processors and operating systems. Each implementation published mappings between local codes and registry identities, including unrepresentable entries, substitutions, and private-use positions.</p><p>Conformance tests compared decoded identity, record boundaries, control handling, and required round trips. A successful exchange did not imply binary compatibility or identical sorting. Data structure, numeric representation, file organization, and peripheral commands remained separately declared.</p>"},
      {id:"presentation",title:"Vertical and horizontal presentation",html:"<p>Registry entries recorded whether a character normally remained upright, rotated, substituted, or changed spacing in vertical composition. Japanese programs and documents could proceed from top to bottom in columns ordered from right to left. Horizontal windows served Latin text, tables, diagrams, and imported material.</p><p>Presentation properties guided terminals and printers without redefining character identity. Low-cost devices used a single orientation or substituted a simpler form. High-end terminals stored multiple glyphs and selected the appropriate form from layout context.</p>"},
      {id:"terminals",title:"Terminals and printers",html:"<p>During the later 1960s and 1970s, mixed-script terminals combined keyboard shifts, dictionary lookup, token conversion, font memories, and portrait display. Printers ranged from kana devices to matrix and photographic systems capable of large registered repertoires.</p><p>A terminal could accept a katakana programming keyword and render its compact token as kanji. General text input expanded more slowly through technical dictionaries, personal-name tables, and selection from candidate lists. Font and dictionary manufacture became a substantial electronics and publishing industry.</p>"},
      {id:"programming",title:"Programming and runtime use",html:"<p>KEISAN, TEJUN, and later Gakushū implementations used registry identities for string data, comments, identifiers, reports, and terminal interaction. Language keywords could remain compact tokens. A compiler documented which characters were legal in identifiers and how source was normalized.</p><p>Foreign-language software required an import profile for character input, filenames, string ordering, and output. Cross-compilers and gateways separated source identity from the target machine's local encoding. Programs still had to account for limited fonts and devices.</p>"},
      {id:"administration",title:"Administrative use",html:"<p>The registry carried personal names, addresses, ship and cargo records, customs declarations, bank accounts, government forms, technical manuals, and interregional messages. Stable identities reduced the need to assign a new proprietary number whenever a record crossed from one corporate installation to another.</p><p>National offices maintained local forms and dictionaries. Central Japanese systems received registered records for trade, statistics, transport, and planning. Paper images and manual review persisted where rare names, damaged text, or legal orthography exceeded the implemented repertoire.</p>"},
      {id:"regional",title:"Regional implementation",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Region or script</th><th>Characteristic implementation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Japan</td><td>Compact kana core, extensive kanji fonts, vertical terminals, and corporate mappings.</td></tr><tr><td>Philippines</td><td>Latin supplement, English and Filipino records, horizontal text, and Japanese control vocabulary.</td></tr><tr><td>China and Manchoukuo</td><td>Traditional Chinese registries, Japanese administrative mappings, and compatibility forms.</td></tr><tr><td>Chōsen</td><td>Japanese administrative core with registered Hangul for local text under restricted language conditions.</td></tr><tr><td>Thailand, Burma, and Indochina</td><td>National-script tables, local forms, and Japanese gateway systems of uneven availability.</td></tr><tr><td>Malay and Indonesian areas</td><td>Latin orthographies, local names, trade records, and Japanese technical profiles.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"language-policy",title:"Language policy",html:"<p>Registration made local scripts usable in modern databases, printing, communications, and technical systems. It did not give associated states equal control over processors, compilers, networks, or standard-setting. Japanese remained the principal language of central documentation, advanced programming education, and interregional technical administration.</p><p>Support also varied by cost. A script could possess registered identities while a provincial office lacked the fonts, keyboard, trained staff, or storage needed for routine use. Transliteration, numeric identifiers, and paper records continued beside multilingual systems.</p>"},
      {id:"defects",title:"Compatibility characters and extensions",html:"<p>The registry accumulated duplicate ideographs, legacy values, corporate extensions, politically preferred forms, and special-purpose characters. Some entries represented distinct historical or administrative practices; others survived because removing them would break installed files.</p><p>Conversion tables could be reversible only when both systems distinguished the same identities. A receiving system preserved an unknown registry number when possible and recorded substitutions when it could not. Private extensions required an identified vendor and version.</p>"},
      {id:"collation",title:"Normalization and collation",html:"<p>Each implementation named its collation rather than assuming one universal alphabetic order. Japanese kana, Chinese dictionary order, Korean, Latin, Thai, and administrative-number sorting required different tables. Character identity did not determine pronunciation or linguistic order.</p><p>Permitted glyph variants remained distinct from identity aliases. Visually similar ideographs were not automatically equal. Normalization rules were awkward where vendor codes or historical records had encoded a preferred form as a separate character.</p>"},
      {id:"maturity",title:"Maturity by 1970",html:"<p>By 1970 the registry had become a practical multibyte interchange system. Government and corporate networks used it for selected production exchanges, while local encodings remained common. Mixed-script printers and terminals expanded during the 1970s as integrated circuits reduced the price of font memory and display control.</p><p>The system supported imperial transaction networks, shipping, banking, industrial logistics, and technical publishing. It also supplied the installed mappings and specialist personnel later brought into international character-standard negotiations.</p>"},
      {id:"unicode",title:"From Kyoto to UNICODE 80",html:"<p>Japan's registry experience informed the 1977 Kyoto universal-character draft. The 1978–79 Geneva compromise widened the process beyond the Japanese sphere, and the 1980 Tokyo–Melbourne congress published <a href='unicode-80.html'>UNICODE 80</a> through the International Character and Data Standards Council.</p><p>UNICODE 80 adopted sixteen-bit abstract identities, extensible registration, and presentation properties while negotiating a more general block order. Japanese and sphere systems mapped existing characters into the new register. Compact local encodings remained valid where they preserved the universal identities.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy",html:"<p>The Co-Prosperity Sphere Common Character Code remained in archival files, industrial systems, national gateways, and corporate equipment after 1980. Its title continued to describe a family of registry and interchange revisions rather than one universally installed byte format.</p><p>Its durable contribution was the separation of character identity, local storage, and visible glyph. UNICODE 80 extended that model into a cross-bloc institution, while the earlier registry retained importance for conversion and the history of Japanese multilingual computing.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"imperial-kana-machine-code.html",kicker:"Japanese predecessor",label:"Imperial Kana Machine Code"},{href:"unicode-80.html",kicker:"Universal successor",label:"UNICODE 80"},{href:"japanese-programming-languages.html",kicker:"Software context",label:"Japanese Programming Languages"},{href:"geacps.html",kicker:"Regional system",label:"Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere"},{href:"philippines.html",kicker:"Latin-script catalyst",label:"Philippines"},{href:"china.html",kicker:"Traditional Chinese use",label:"National Republic of China"},{href:"korean-language.html",kicker:"Hangul and language policy",label:"Korean Language in Chōsen"},{href:"software-portability.html",kicker:"Conversion practice",label:"Software Portability"}],facts:[["Working Japanese title","共栄圏共通文字符号"],["English title","Co-Prosperity Sphere Common Character Code"],["Registry established","1962–63"],["Multibyte interchange","1964–65"],["Practical maturity","By 1970"],["Predecessor","Imperial Kana Machine Code"],["Principal catalyst","Philippine Latin-script requirements"],["Core principle","Stable identity independent of machine or glyph"],["Universal successor","UNICODE 80"],["Exact code assignments","Open"]]
  }),

  "keisan": softwareArticle({
    slug:"keisan",title:"KEISAN",eyebrow:"Japanese scientific programming language · from 1958",infoboxKicker:"Japanese scientific-language family",
    lead:"<strong>KEISAN</strong>—written ケイサン and later 計算—is Japan's principal scientific and engineering programming-language family. Created in 1958–59 as a portable formula language, it developed through the numbered 計算 standards into the normal Japanese environment for numerical analysis, matrices, instruments, engineering graphics, spacecraft, robotics, and large scientific libraries. Its <a href='japanese-vertical-computing.html'>native source is vertical</a>, its arrays follow mathematical convention, and its standardized language can be compiled for otherwise incompatible corporate machines throughout the <a href='geacps.html'>Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere</a>.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>",
    canon:"The 1958–59 origin and portable core; the 1962, 1964, 1968, 1972, 1976, and 1982 revisions; the corrected imperial-year names 計算二二, 計算二四, 計算二八, 計算三二, 計算三六, and 計算四二; the static type and array model; native source conventions; modules, files, graphics, instrument services, cross-compilation, and the later space, robotics, and parallel-numerics profiles are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Exact grammars, compiler organizations, vendor market shares, and complete standard-library catalogues remain open. The superseded labels KEISAN 62/64/68/72 are not canonical foreign names.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origin",title:"Origins",html:"<p>Japanese scientific programming before KEISAN consisted of numeric machine orders, symbolic addresses, mathematical notations, and incompatible manufacturer conventions. Relay and parametron laboratories, telecommunications establishments, punched-card departments, and university computing groups shared routines on paper or tape, but a program prepared for one machine normally required substantial rewriting for another.</p><p>KEISAN appeared around 1958–59. FACOM, the Electrotechnical Laboratory, NEC, and university groups first produced related but incompatible compilers. A national specification issued in 1959 defined a portable core rather than one mandatory implementation. Early compilers generally produced less efficient code than contemporary American FORTRAN compilers, but the common source allowed institutions to retain calculations and teaching material when equipment changed.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"core",title:"The 1959 portable core",html:"<p>The core language provided floating-point arithmetic, formulas, fixed arrays, loops, conditions, procedures, mathematical libraries, and formatted numerical input and output. Representative control words included モシ for a condition, ソノタ for an alternative, クリカエシ for repetition, ヨミコミ for reading, カキダシ for writing, and オワリ for the end of a unit.</p><p>First-generation source could mix Latin mathematical formulas, short Latin or kana identifiers, katakana control words, Arabic numerals, and standard scientific function names. Punched cards and paper tape were normal source media. The portable specification defined common language behavior; each compiler documented machine word size, numeric range, available storage, peripheral conventions, and departures from the core.</p>"},
      {id:"names",title:"Names and numbering",html:"<p>Japanese official and domestic references use the title 計算 with the last two digits of the imperial year written in Japanese numerals. Foreign explanatory literature may romanize the same suffix. The 1962 standard is therefore 計算二二 (<em>Keisan 22</em>), not “KEISAN 62.” The first portable core predates the numbered sequence and is usually identified by its 1959 date.</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Introduction</th><th>Domestic short form</th><th>Formal style</th><th>Foreign form</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1962</td><td>計算二二</td><td>二二式計算言語</td><td>Keisan 22</td></tr><tr><td>1964</td><td>計算二四</td><td>二四式計算言語</td><td>Keisan 24</td></tr><tr><td>1968</td><td>計算二八</td><td>二八式計算言語</td><td>Keisan 28</td></tr><tr><td>1972</td><td>計算三二</td><td>三二式計算言語</td><td>Keisan 32</td></tr><tr><td>1976</td><td>計算三六</td><td>三六式計算言語</td><td>Keisan 36</td></tr><tr><td>1982</td><td>計算四二</td><td>四二式計算言語</td><td>Keisan 42</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The corrected imperial-year forms apply retroactively in catalogues, standards histories, and technical writing.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"chronology",title:"Development",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Generation</th><th>Principal additions</th><th>Characteristic use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Portable core, 1959</td><td>Floating point, arrays, loops, procedures, formulas, libraries, and formatted output.</td><td>University, laboratory, and engineering batch calculation.</td></tr><tr><td>計算二二, 1962</td><td>Vertical statements, Japanese positional numerals, katakana identifiers, and native scientific source.</td><td>Japanese mainframes and vertical-source installations.</td></tr><tr><td>計算二四, 1964</td><td>Blocks, separate procedures, records, limited recursion, checks, complex and matrix libraries, and device-independent input/output.</td><td>Larger scientific programs and portable institutional libraries.</td></tr><tr><td>計算二八, 1968</td><td>Restricted procedure values, modules, scientific files, graphics, error handling, and improved optimization.</td><td>Aerospace, research, engineering graphics, and production science.</td></tr><tr><td>計算三二, 1972</td><td>Tagged records, plotting, instrument services, compiler diagnostics, and small-target cross-compilation.</td><td>Laboratory instruments, minicomputers, and embedded scientific equipment.</td></tr><tr><td>計算三六, 1976</td><td>Stronger professional modules, numerical libraries, instrument control, graphics, and universal-character mappings.</td><td>Mature professional engineering systems.</td></tr><tr><td>計算四二, 1982</td><td>Sixteen- and thirty-two-bit profiles, parallel numerical packages, universal-character data, spacecraft, and robotics.</td><td>Advanced automation, space systems, and networked scientific work.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"keisan22",title:"計算二二",html:"<p>計算二二 of 1962 stabilized fully native Japanese scientific source. Statements were arranged vertically, identifiers could be written in katakana, and numeric literals used Japanese positional digits. Arrays, loops, conditions, procedures, scientific functions, and formatted output from the portable core remained available. A conforming program required no Latin characters.</p><p>The standard accompanied the first expensive portrait-oriented terminals, though it could also be prepared on cards or paper tape and printed as reconstructed vertical columns. Cheaper equipment sometimes produced a horizontal transliteration for checking and storage. Such listings were compatibility products rather than the preferred representation of the program.</p>"},
      {id:"keisan24",title:"計算二四",html:"<p>計算二四 of 1964 introduced lexical blocks, separately compiled procedures, named record forms, limited recursion, stronger array checks, standardized complex arithmetic, matrix libraries, and device-independent scientific input and output. Optional Latin identifiers were available through a supplementary profile for imported programs and multinational work.</p><p>Separate compilation allowed a laboratory to maintain tested mathematical procedures independently of the programs that called them. It did not create binary compatibility among Japanese machines: each compiler produced object modules for its own processor and operating environment. Source compatibility and declared interfaces remained the common layer.</p>"},
      {id:"keisan28",title:"計算二八",html:"<p>計算二八 of 1968 became the principal standardized scientific language. It added procedures as values in restricted contexts, separately compiled modules, scientific data files, graphics and plotting packages, standardized error handling, and improved compiler optimization. Blocks, complex numbers, and matrix facilities from 計算二四 were retained.</p><p>Modules published typed procedure and data signatures. Implementations could check a call without exposing the module's internal representation. Scientific files carried declared record and numeric formats, allowing laboratories to exchange measurements and computed results through conversion software even when their native machines differed.</p>"},
      {id:"keisan32",title:"計算三二",html:"<p>計算三二 of 1972 added tagged record forms, improved matrix and statistical libraries, plotting, instrument services, standardized compiler diagnostics, and cross-compilation for small processors. Tagged records allowed a value to carry a declared case together with the fields appropriate to that case, a useful form for instrument results and heterogeneous observations.</p><p>Cross-compilers ran on a larger host and produced code for minicomputers or specialized processors with less memory. Unsupported facilities were diagnosed for the selected target. This practice allowed one language and library culture to extend from central scientific machines into instruments, vehicles, and industrial equipment.</p>"},
      {id:"keisan36",title:"計算三六",html:"<p>Introduced in 1976, 計算三六 was the mature professional scientific revision. It strengthened modules, instrument control, numerical libraries, cross-compilation, mixed vertical and horizontal page regions, graphics, plotting, and mappings to the developing universal-character system.</p><p>計算三六 remained a professional language taught through universities, corporate laboratories, and technical institutes. It was too elaborate for ordinary household learners; the educational and consumer role passed to <a href='gakushu.html'>学習三八</a> after 1978. Scientific organizations continued to use 計算三六 source and libraries after newer compilers appeared.</p>"},
      {id:"keisan42",title:"計算四二",html:"<p>計算四二 (<em>Keisan 42</em>) appeared in 1982 for sixteen- and thirty-two-bit systems. It supplied stronger modules, complex and matrix libraries, graphics, scientific-instrument interfaces, parallel numerical packages, universal-character data, and cross-compilation profiles for spacecraft and robotics processors.</p><p>Parallel computation was presented through libraries operating on arrays and declared worker groups. The language did not provide unrestricted shared-memory semantics. A program selected a numerical operation and a group of workers; the library and implementation managed division, synchronization, and collection according to the target profile.</p>"},
      {id:"source",title:"Source representation",html:"<p>Native KEISAN source is a serialized stream of logical tokens and layout records. Its canonical editor view begins in the upper-right of a portrait page, advances downward through a statement, and continues in the next column to the left. Nested blocks may appear as inset vertical bands. Diagnostics and annotations can occupy an adjacent column.</p><p>Horizontal left-to-right regions accommodate matrices, graphs, waveforms, imported formulas, and Latin-script records. A mixed page remains one ordered source stream rather than a two-dimensional image. The same program can therefore be stored, transmitted, and compiled on systems whose displays differ substantially.</p>"},
      {id:"characters",title:"Characters, tokens, and numerals",html:"<p>Early compact systems used the <a href='imperial-kana-machine-code.html'>Imperial Kana Machine Code</a>. The later <a href='co-prosperity-sphere-common-character-code.html'>Co-Prosperity Sphere Common Character Code</a> and <a href='unicode-80.html'>UNICODE 80</a> expanded multilingual interchange and preserved character identity independently of a particular manufacturer's storage code.</p><p>Common language words are stored as tokens. A simple printer may render a token in katakana while a capable terminal shows an approved kanji form; both compile identically. Native numeric literals use 〇一二三四五六七八九, which map directly to values. Arabic digits and Latin identifiers belong to import or supplementary profiles rather than the required native language.</p>"},
      {id:"types",title:"Types and records",html:"<p>KEISAN is statically typed. Its base types are integer, real, complex, truth, character, and fixed array. Implementations publish the supported ranges and representations for each selected machine. Complex arithmetic is standardized in 計算二四, together with named records used to group measurements, parameters, and descriptive data.</p><p>計算三二 adds tagged variants. A tag identifies which declared alternative a record contains, permitting compilers and checking tools to reject access to fields that are absent from that case. These forms are used for instrument readings, scientific file records, and interfaces whose result may have several defined shapes.</p>"},
      {id:"arrays",title:"Arrays and numerical libraries",html:"<p>Arrays begin at one and are stored in column-major order, matching the matrix convention used by Japanese scientific libraries. Bounds are fixed by the declaration or target profile. Later compilers perform stronger static and run-time checks where the machine and installation policy permit them.</p><p>Standard and institutional libraries cover elementary functions, complex arithmetic, matrices, statistics, plotting, numerical methods, and instrument conversion. Libraries accumulated across several generations, and a compiler commonly offered both a strict current profile and compatibility modes for established source. Individual implementations could omit a package when their storage or peripheral resources were inadequate.</p>"},
      {id:"procedures",title:"Procedures and modules",html:"<p>Procedures belong to the 1959 core. 計算二四 adds lexical blocks, separately compiled procedures, and limited recursion. Recursion uses a stack when the target provides one; a compiler for a small processor diagnoses the facility as unsupported rather than silently changing its behavior.</p><p>計算二八 introduces modules with typed procedure and data signatures and permits procedure values in restricted contexts. Later revisions strengthen versioning and cross-compilation of professional modules. Laboratories and corporate engineering offices use these interfaces to maintain validated routines separately from application programs.</p>"},
      {id:"io",title:"Files, instruments, and graphics",html:"<p>計算二四 separates scientific input and output from a specific card reader, tape unit, or printer. 計算二八 adds scientific files, graphics, and plotting packages. 計算三二 and 計算三六 extend the model to named instrument services, so that a program can request a measurement, calibration, trace, graph, or output operation through a documented interface.</p><p>Device independence is bounded by each installation's supported classes. A compiler or library maps the common operation to a local driver, conversion unit, or procedure. Hardware-specific timing, accuracy, and failure behavior remain documented by the equipment maker and laboratory.</p>"},
      {id:"errors",title:"Checks, diagnostics, and optimization",html:"<p>Array checks and structured diagnostics expanded as programs grew. 計算二八 standardized classes of error and improved optimization; 計算三二 regularized compiler diagnostics for host and cross-target builds. Installations could select checks appropriate to development, production calculation, or a restricted processor.</p><p>Optimization remained implementation-dependent. Manufacturers competed on generated code, mathematical libraries, and adaptation to their own processors while conforming to common source behavior. Program reports therefore named the compiler, edition, target profile, library set, and relevant numeric assumptions alongside the KEISAN language generation.</p>"},
      {id:"space",title:"Aerospace, spacecraft, and robotics",html:"<p>KEISAN entered aerospace work through numerical analysis, trajectories, structural calculation, test instrumentation, graphics, and cross-compilation. A large host could calculate models and also produce bounded software for a smaller flight or instrument processor. The selected target profile determined available arithmetic, storage, recursion, and device interfaces.</p><p>計算四二 formalized spacecraft and robotics profiles. It supported universal-character records, instruments, matrices, graphics, and cross-target modules alongside parallel numerical packages for ground analysis. Process persistence, authorization, lost-link states, and supervised autonomy remained primarily the province of <a href='tejun.html'>TEJUN</a>; projects could use both families for different parts of one system.</p>"},
      {id:"institutions",title:"Institutions and sphere use",html:"<p>Universities, state laboratories, observatories, zaibatsu research divisions, shipyards, aerospace bodies, and industrial engineering offices maintained KEISAN compilers and libraries. Technical schools trained scientific programmers, while manufacturers supplied machine-specific implementations, conversion tools, and peripheral packages.</p><p>Associated-state institutes used the common specification through Japanese-language training and local character supplements. The Philippines increased demand for Latin-script records and interchange, while Chinese, Korean, and Southeast Asian data encouraged the separation of language tokens from displayed characters. The resulting environment remained Japanese-led and institutionally controlled rather than an unrestricted common software market.</p>"},
      {id:"relations",title:"Related language families",html:"<p>KEISAN shares native character, source-layout, object, and conversion practices with other <a href='japanese-programming-languages.html'>Japanese programming languages</a>. TEJUN handles administration, transactions, industrial processes, and persistent control. Gakushū supplies the smaller interpreted environment used in homes and schools. Research languages developed during the early 1980s for functional, logic, and graphics work without displacing the principal families.</p><p>Imported FORTRAN, ALGOL, and other scientific source could be translated or compiled under supplementary profiles. Cross-bloc exchange usually passed through explicit character conversion, numeric declarations, and library replacement. KEISAN's portable source specification did not imply object-code compatibility with foreign or domestic processors.</p>"},
      {id:"position",title:"Position in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985, KEISAN comprised more than a single compiler or grammar. The name covered a standards lineage, compatibility profiles, manufacturer implementations, numerical libraries, instrument interfaces, source conventions, and a professional community extending across Japanese and sphere institutions.</p><p>計算四二 was the current scientific revision. Older programs in 計算三二 and 計算三六 remained in service where their libraries and target machines were validated. The long-lived one-origin array model, vertical source, typed module interfaces, and cross-compilation practice gave the family continuity from batch calculations to advanced automation.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"japanese-programming-languages.html",kicker:"National context",label:"Japanese Programming Languages"},{href:"tejun.html",kicker:"Administrative and control family",label:"TEJUN"},{href:"gakushu.html",kicker:"Educational and home family",label:"Gakushū"},{href:"imperial-kana-machine-code.html",kicker:"Early source encoding",label:"Imperial Kana Machine Code"},{href:"co-prosperity-sphere-common-character-code.html",kicker:"Sphere interchange",label:"Common Character Code"},{href:"unicode-80.html",kicker:"Universal character standard",label:"UNICODE 80"},{href:"software-portability.html",kicker:"Cross-machine practice",label:"Software Portability"},{href:"computing.html",kicker:"Technological overview",label:"Computing"}],facts:[["First implementations","1958–59"],["Portable core","1959"],["First numbered standard","計算二二 (1962)"],["Current 1985 revision","計算四二 (1982)"],["Foreign forms","Keisan 22, 24, 28, 32, 36, 42"],["Typing","Static"],["Array origin","One"],["Array order","Column-major"],["Native layout","Vertical, top-to-bottom; columns right-to-left"],["Primary domain","Science and engineering"],["1982 focus","16/32-bit systems, space, robotics, parallel numerics"]]
  }),

  "tejun": softwareArticle({
    slug:"tejun",title:"TEJUN",eyebrow:"Japanese administrative and control language · from 1959",infoboxKicker:"Persistent procedure language family",
    lead:"TEJUN—written テジュン and later 手順—is Japan's principal programming-language family for administration, transactions, factories, devices, and supervised autonomous systems. A TEJUN program describes persistent typed processes moving among named states in response to events. Deadlines, exception routes, checkpoints, acknowledgements, and explicit human authority are part of the program model. The family grew from clerical and industrial procedure sheets into a common software layer for otherwise incompatible Japanese corporate machines.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>",
    canon:"The 1959–60 translator origin; the 1963, 1965, 1969, 1973, 1977, and 1983 generations; the corrected imperial-year names 手順二三, 手順二五, 手順二九, 手順三三, 手順三七, and 手順四三; persistent-process semantics; transaction and restart rules; embedded limits; and spacecraft and robotics profiles are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Exact surface grammar, compiler organizations, vendor dialect shares, and full standard libraries remain open. The superseded labels TEJUN 63/65/69/73 are not canonical foreign names.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origin",title:"From procedure sheets to translators",html:"<p>TEJUN descended from standardized forms used to describe clerical and industrial work. A sheet named a step, input, responsible clerk or machine, expected output, exception route, and required authorization. Workers, cards, relays, electronic equipment, or a mixed installation could follow the same document.</p><p>The first translators appeared around 1959–60. They provided records, exact decimal fields, tables, sorting, files, device operations, states, exceptions, and explicit human approval. Corporate implementations differed in syntax and machine interface, while the common specification described portable process and record behavior.</p>"},
      {id:"names",title:"Names and numbering",html:"<p>Japanese official and domestic references use the title 手順 with the last two digits of the imperial year written in Japanese numerals. Foreign explanatory literature uses a romanized form with the same suffix. The 1963 standard is therefore 手順二三 (*Tejun 23*), not “TEJUN 63.”</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Introduction</th><th>Domestic short form</th><th>Formal style</th><th>Foreign form</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1963</td><td>手順二三</td><td>二三式手順言語</td><td>Tejun 23</td></tr><tr><td>1965</td><td>手順二五</td><td>二五式手順言語</td><td>Tejun 25</td></tr><tr><td>1969</td><td>手順二九</td><td>二九式手順言語</td><td>Tejun 29</td></tr><tr><td>1973</td><td>手順三三</td><td>三三式手順言語</td><td>Tejun 33</td></tr><tr><td>1977</td><td>手順三七</td><td>三七式手順言語</td><td>Tejun 37</td></tr><tr><td>1983</td><td>手順四三</td><td>四三式手順言語</td><td>Tejun 43</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The first 1959–60 translators predated this numbered national sequence.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"chronology",title:"Development",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Generation</th><th>Principal additions</th><th>Typical installations</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>First translators, 1959–60</td><td>Records, decimals, tables, sorting, files, devices, states, exceptions, and approval.</td><td>Trading houses, government offices, warehouses, and early industrial systems.</td></tr><tr><td>手順二三, 1963</td><td>Common record forms, file descriptions, state tables, device operations, and procedure documentation.</td><td>Corporate mainframes and supervised process systems.</td></tr><tr><td>手順二五, 1965</td><td>Events, timers, queues, restart states, batches, confirmation, authorization gates, and persistent records.</td><td>Ports, refineries, exchanges, railways, power, and production.</td></tr><tr><td>手順二九, 1969</td><td>Cooperating procedures, rollback, parallel cells, messages, and maintenance states.</td><td>Factory networks and imperial transaction systems.</td></tr><tr><td>手順三三, 1973</td><td>Nested processes, event priority, device families, acknowledgements, distributed calls, and bounded embedded profiles.</td><td>Distributed industry, transport, and advanced equipment.</td></tr><tr><td>手順三七, 1977</td><td>Factory-network transactions, safe restart, terminal forms, authorization, and corporate file exchange.</td><td>Networked factories, offices, and regional corporate systems.</td></tr><tr><td>手順四三, 1983</td><td>Concurrent procedures, timeouts, lost-link states, reservations, supervised autonomy, and explicit safe states.</td><td>Spacecraft, robots, remote stations, and mature industrial networks.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"tejun23",title:"手順二三 and 手順二五",html:"<p>手順二三 of 1963 standardized record layouts, decimal fields, files, sorting, state tables, named device actions, operator approval, exception routes, and procedure-sheet documentation. It gave offices and factories a common source model while leaving storage media and peripheral details to each implementation.</p><p>手順二五 of 1965 added asynchronous events, timers, message queues, restart states, production batches, transaction confirmation, authority gates, and persistent industrial records. It became important in warehouses, ports, refineries, telephone exchanges, rail terminals, trading-house accounts, carrier maintenance, and power dispatch.</p>"},
      {id:"tejun29",title:"手順二九",html:"<p>手順二九 of 1969 made cooperating procedures a normal language construct. Processes exchanged messages, entered parallel production cells, rolled back prepared work, and moved equipment into named maintenance states. Event-driven control could remain active while a separate administrative procedure awaited records or authorization.</p><p>The generation supported factory networks and hierarchical imperial transactions. It did not assume an open packet network or a single shared database. Each participant retained its own durable state and communicated through declared messages.</p>"},
      {id:"tejun33",title:"手順三三",html:"<p>手順三三 of 1973 added nested processes, priorities, device families, acknowledgements, safe rollback, distributed calls, and explicit distinctions among operator, supervisor, and remote authority. A device family allowed one procedure to address conforming pumps, cranes, terminals, or controllers through a documented interface.</p><p>The embedded profile fixed storage, queue size, task count, and timing assumptions. Recursion and dynamic allocation were prohibited on bounded targets. Cross-compilers produced code for smaller industrial and vehicle processors from central development systems.</p>"},
      {id:"tejun37",title:"手順三七",html:"<p>Introduced in 1977, 手順三七 strengthened distributed transactions, factory-network messaging, safe process restart, local and remote authorization, maintenance-state tracking, terminal forms, and corporate file exchange. Standard forms connected screen fields to typed records and validation rules.</p><p>The language was used across installations that combined mainframes, minicomputers, terminals, and embedded controllers. Gateways translated character encodings and records while preserving process acknowledgements and audit information.</p>"},
      {id:"tejun43",title:"手順四三",html:"<p>手順四三 of 1983 supported concurrent procedures, acknowledged messages, timeouts, lost-link states, transaction recovery, resource reservations, local and remote authorization, supervised autonomy, and explicit safe states. It served spacecraft, robots, distributed Mars systems, and remote industrial equipment.</p><p>A machine could continue a bounded operation after losing communications. It could not redefine its mission authority. The declared lost-link state specified permitted tasks, resource limits, retry schedules, and the condition requiring shutdown or human review.</p>"},
      {id:"source",title:"Source representation",html:"<p><a href='japanese-vertical-computing.html'>TEJUN source is vertical</a> by default. Characters descend within a statement and successive columns proceed from right to left. Horizontal windows are used for tables, diagrams, imported records, and Latin-script material. The stored program is a token stream with layout records.</p><p><a href='imperial-kana-machine-code.html'>Imperial Kana Machine Code</a> supplied the early compact repertoire. The <a href='co-prosperity-sphere-common-character-code.html'>sphere common-character system</a> and later <a href='unicode-80.html'>UNICODE 80</a> supported multilingual records and display. Katakana and kanji renderings of the same language token compiled identically.</p>"},
      {id:"data",title:"Data and records",html:"<p>Typed records are the principal durable data structure. Fields may contain exact decimal quantities, integers, truth values, characters, enumerated states, dates, times, identifiers, and references to files or devices. Fixed layouts support tape, disk, terminal forms, and interdepartmental exchange.</p><p>Money and measured quantities declare scale and rounding. Files may be sequential, indexed, journaled, or represented by bounded nonvolatile slots in embedded systems. Each implementation documents character encoding, collation, numeric limits, media behavior, and record conversion.</p>"},
      {id:"process",title:"Persistent process records",html:"<p>A TEJUN program contains persistent typed process records. Each process declares its possible states, accepted events, guards, actions, next states, deadline, failure state, and required authority. Its state survives ordinary suspension and restart.</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>State</td><td>Names the current stage of work or equipment condition.</td></tr><tr><td>Event</td><td>Reports an arrival, message, timer, operator action, or device change.</td></tr><tr><td>Guard</td><td>Tests whether a transition may occur.</td></tr><tr><td>Action</td><td>Changes records, sends a message, or commands a declared device.</td></tr><tr><td>Next state</td><td>Commits the process's new durable condition.</td></tr><tr><td>Deadline</td><td>Produces a timeout event when confirmation does not arrive.</td></tr><tr><td>Failure state</td><td>Defines recoverable or safe behavior.</td></tr><tr><td>Authority</td><td>Specifies who may permit a protected transition.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"events",title:"Events and scheduling",html:"<p>Events are queued first by declared priority and then by arrival order. A transition executes atomically with respect to its own process record. Another process does not observe a partly committed local state.</p><p>Timers, messages, operator input, sensor changes, and device completion can all produce events. Queue bounds and overflow behavior are part of an embedded profile. Priority does not confer authority; a high-priority event may still wait at a protected gate.</p>"},
      {id:"transactions",title:"Transactions and acknowledgement",html:"<p>Multi-process work uses prepare and confirm messages. A participant reserves resources or prepares a change, reports readiness, and commits only after the required confirmation. This is an explicit protocol rather than an invisible shared-memory transaction.</p><p>A checkpoint commits process state, durable output, and an acknowledgement number together. Messages include identities that allow a receiver to recognize repetition. Later profiles add timeout, cancellation, reconciliation, and safe rollback rules.</p>"},
      {id:"restart",title:"Restart and reconciliation",html:"<p>After a failure, the runtime restores the last checkpoint and examines unacknowledged work. An idempotent action may be repeated when its profile says that repetition has the same permitted effect. A non-idempotent device action requires an operator reconciliation record before the process proceeds.</p><p>Reconciliation may compare device position, material count, local log, remote acknowledgement, and the last authorized command. Maintenance personnel can move the process into a declared repair state and record the disposition of incomplete work.</p>"},
      {id:"embedded",title:"Embedded profile",html:"<p>Embedded TEJUN replaces general persistent files with fixed nonvolatile slots and bounded queues. It prohibits recursion and dynamic allocation. The profile declares memory use, event capacity, timers, device registers, deadlines, and the machine-code interface before the controller enters service.</p><p>Small targets may omit terminal forms, general sorting, and large character tables. They retain the state, event, checkpoint, authority, and safe-failure model required by their installation.</p>"},
      {id:"devices",title:"Devices and maintenance",html:"<p>Named devices expose commands, status events, failure reports, and maintenance modes. A process can reserve a device, issue an allowed operation, await acknowledgement, release the reservation, or route an exception to an operator station.</p><p>Maintenance states isolate equipment from normal production and record who authorized return to service. Device families support common procedures across compatible machinery while allowing manufacturer-specific adapters.</p>"},
      {id:"authority",title:"Human authority",html:"<p>TEJUN distinguishes ordinary operator, supervisor, local technical authority, and remote authority where an installation requires them. Protected transitions name the credential or confirmation needed. The runtime records the approving identity and time with the transaction.</p><p>Human approval is used for unusual financial transfers, safety overrides, maintenance release, changes of production mode, and mission-level decisions. Automated procedures continue within declared limits while responsibility for specified decisions remains assigned to an office or person.</p>"},
      {id:"applications",title:"Applications",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Representative work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Administration</td><td>Government records, forms, approvals, reporting, and interoffice files.</td></tr><tr><td>Commerce</td><td>Trading-house accounts, banking, clearing, inventory, and settlement.</td></tr><tr><td>Transport and logistics</td><td>Ports, shipping manifests, warehouses, railway terminals, and carrier maintenance.</td></tr><tr><td>Industry</td><td>Production schedules, machine tools, rolling mills, refineries, quality control, and maintenance.</td></tr><tr><td>Infrastructure</td><td>Telephone exchanges, power dispatch, communications, and terminal networks.</td></tr><tr><td>Military systems</td><td>Logistics, air-defense support, naval maintenance, communications, and production control.</td></tr><tr><td>Space and robotics</td><td>Distributed spacecraft, rovers, stations, resource reservations, and supervised autonomy.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"sphere",title:"Use across the Co-Prosperity Sphere",html:"<p>Japanese corporations and government bodies supplied TEJUN compilers, procedure standards, and training across the <a href='geacps.html'>Co-Prosperity Sphere</a>. Local institutions adapted record forms, devices, dictionaries, and reports. Japanese technical vocabulary and compiler documentation remained dominant.</p><p><a href='philippines.html'>Philippine</a> programmers became important in mixed-script commerce, shipping, banking, and conversion to American formats. Other associated states used national-script tables through the sphere character registry. Access to advanced compilers, processors, and libraries remained uneven.</p>"},
      {id:"space",title:"Spacecraft and robotics",html:"<p>TEJUN process concepts fitted systems composed of orbiters, relays, landers, rovers, weather stations, cargo vehicles, and crewed facilities. Each machine exchanged state and task messages while retaining its own durable record and bounded failure behavior.</p><p>手順四三 supported the automated crewed orbital program and distributed Mars operations by 1985. KEISAN supplied trajectories, structures, sensors, and scientific computation; TEJUN coordinated messages, reservations, equipment state, recovery, and human authorization.</p>"},
      {id:"relation",title:"Relation to other languages",html:"<p><a href='keisan.html'>KEISAN</a> remained the principal Japanese scientific and engineering language. TEJUN handled records, processes, devices, and transactions. The families could call generated routines or exchange declared records without sharing one runtime.</p><p>COBOL competed in international business, while German RP-S and American JOVIAL-class systems occupied parts of the real-time field. TEJUN's persistent states, authority gates, and restart model remained distinct. <a href='gakushu.html'>Gakushū</a> served home and school users rather than production control.</p>"},
      {id:"position",title:"Position in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 TEJUN was Japan's most important administrative and industrial language family. Six numbered generations supported corporate mainframes, terminals, factory networks, embedded controllers, spacecraft, and robots. Older installations remained in service beside newer distributed profiles.</p><p>Its standards defined source and process behavior across different corporate machines. Object code, device adapters, file encodings, and operating environments remained vendor-specific.</p>"}
    ],related:[...japaneseSoftwareRelated,{href:"imperial-kana-machine-code.html",kicker:"Early source encoding",label:"Imperial Kana Machine Code"},{href:"co-prosperity-sphere-common-character-code.html",kicker:"Multilingual records",label:"Sphere Common Character Code"},{href:"unicode-80.html",kicker:"Universal character identity",label:"UNICODE 80"},{href:"geacps.html",kicker:"Regional system",label:"Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere"},{href:"philippines.html",kicker:"Mixed-script implementation",label:"Philippines"},{href:"software-portability.html",kicker:"Cross-machine practice",label:"Software Portability"}],facts:[["First translators","1959–60"],["Major generations","手順二三 · 二五 · 二九 · 三三 · 三七 · 四三"],["Foreign suffixes","Tejun 23, 25, 29, 33, 37, 43"],["Core unit","Persistent typed process"],["Control model","States, events, guards, actions"],["Durability","Checkpoint and acknowledgement"],["Transaction model","Prepare and confirm messages"],["Embedded limits","No recursion or dynamic allocation"],["Authority","Operator, supervisor, local, and remote gates"],["1983 focus","Spacecraft, robotics, and distributed systems"]]
  }),

  "gakushu": softwareArticle({
    slug:"gakushu",title:"Gakushū",eyebrow:"Japanese home and educational language · 1978–1985",infoboxKicker:"Interpreted consumer language family",
    lead:"<strong>Gakushū</strong> (学習, “learning”) is Japan's principal native-script home and educational programming-language family. 学習三八 (<em>Gakushū 38</em>) appeared in 1978 as an interpreted beginner's language for schools and household computers; 学習四一 (<em>Gakushū 41</em>) added universal-character text, disks, communications, structured procedures, and compatibility profiles in 1981. Programs combine immediate interactive operation with <a href='japanese-vertical-computing.html'>vertical source</a>, Japanese numerals, graphics, sound, controllers, cassettes, disks, and cartridges.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>",
    canon:"The imperial-year names 学習三八 and 学習四一; the 1978 and 1981 introductions; interpreted operation; vertical tokenized source; the portable integer, string, array, statement, and workspace model; graphics and sound devices; manufacturer extensions; Philippine localization; and the educational and consumer role are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Exact keyword spellings, national school syllabuses, individual manufacturer dialect shares, hardware brands, and complete cartridge and communications interfaces remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origins",title:"Origins",html:"<p>The expansion of Japanese household computing during the late 1970s created demand for a language smaller and more approachable than professional <a href='keisan.html'>KEISAN</a> or <a href='tejun.html'>TEJUN</a>. Imported BASIC demonstrated immediate commands, short programs, magazine listings, and inexpensive interpreters, but English keywords and horizontal Latin source did not fit the established Japanese character and display system.</p><p>Major electronics firms agreed upon a national educational core while retaining their own machines and extensions. The first standard appeared in 1978 as 学習三八. Its title means “learning,” and the suffix uses the final two digits of the imperial year in Japanese numerals.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"names",title:"Names and revisions",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Introduction</th><th>Domestic name</th><th>Romanization</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1978</td><td>学習三八</td><td>Gakushū 38</td><td>Original home, school, and beginner's standard.</td></tr><tr><td>1981</td><td>学習四一</td><td>Gakushū 41</td><td>Revised consumer standard with UNICODE 80, disks, communications, and compatibility profiles.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Japanese official and domestic references retain the Japanese-language title and imperial-year suffix. Romanization is used in explanatory foreign text. GAKUSHŪ in all capitals is common in foreign catalogues but does not indicate a separate edition.</p>"},
      {id:"chronology",title:"Development",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Development</th><th>Typical setting</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1975–1977</td><td>Consumer microprocessors, television adapters, cassettes, games, compact character displays, and manufacturer beginner dialects establish the market.</td><td>Electronics stores, clubs, demonstrations, and early home systems.</td></tr><tr><td>1978</td><td>学習三八 defines an interpreted native educational core with immediate commands, vertical source, Japanese numerals, graphics, sound, controllers, cassettes, and cartridges.</td><td>Schools, homes, magazines, and technical clubs.</td></tr><tr><td>1979–1980</td><td>Manufacturer profiles, localizations, published listings, games, and peripheral extensions broaden the family.</td><td>Household computers and associated-state editions.</td></tr><tr><td>1981</td><td>学習四一 adds UNICODE 80 text, mixed windows, disks, communications, structured procedures, and compatibility declarations.</td><td>Mature eight- and sixteen-bit consumer systems.</td></tr><tr><td>1982–1985</td><td>Native sixteen-bit personal computers, improved graphics and sound, communications, and a larger commercial software market coexist with console systems.</td><td>Homes, schools, clubs, publishers, and small technical offices.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"g38",title:"学習三八",html:"<p>学習三八 is Japan's counterpart to the international BASIC movement. It offers an immediate command environment, numbered or named program columns, integer and decimal arithmetic, conditions, loops, simple arrays, text, graphics, sound, controllers, cassette storage, and cartridge access. A learner can enter a command, inspect its result, add it to a program, run the program, and return to editing.</p><p>The standard is deliberately small enough for household systems. Manufacturers may omit optional numeric or peripheral facilities, but a conforming machine supplies the common interactive, control-flow, text, and array operations and identifies its installed graphics, sound, storage, and controller capabilities.</p>"},
      {id:"interaction",title:"Interactive operation",html:"<p>The interpreter opens at a command column rather than requiring a separate compiler and linker. Immediate expressions and device commands execute directly. Stored statements can be listed, changed, run, stopped, and resumed according to the machine profile.</p><p>When execution fails, the runtime highlights the vertical statement and returns to the command column without erasing the program. The user can inspect variables, alter a statement, and run the program again. This recovery rule supports experimentation on machines that lack a professional operating environment.</p>"},
      {id:"layout",title:"Vertical source",html:"<p>A Gakushū program begins at the upper-right of its display. Characters descend within a narrow statement column, and later columns appear to the left. On a landscape household television, several vertical columns share the screen; a horizontal strip at the bottom may show status, numeric output, or a device message.</p><p>Professional portrait terminals are unnecessary for ordinary home use. 学習四一 permits vertical and horizontal windows on the same display, allowing a program to retain its native layout while showing a graph, game field, table, or imported record horizontally.</p>"},
      {id:"tokens",title:"Tokens, characters, and numerals",html:"<p>Source is katakana-heavy, tokenized, and written with Japanese positional numerals. The required language contains no Latin characters. A compact machine can display a reserved word in kana; a more capable machine can render the same stored token as an approved kanji form.</p><p>The <a href='co-prosperity-sphere-common-character-code.html'>sphere common-character system</a> supplies earlier character identities. 学習四一 uses <a href='unicode-80.html'>UNICODE 80</a> for text and comments while allowing compact local storage. Program exchange therefore requires both the language profile and the machine's character mapping.</p>"},
      {id:"machine",title:"Portable machine model",html:"<p>The portable Gakushū machine uses sixteen-bit integers, optional decimal floating point, length-counted strings, one-origin arrays, tokenized statements, and a global variable workspace. The common model specifies language behavior rather than one physical processor, memory map, or display controller.</p><p>A program can discover logical graphics and sound devices at startup. Numeric capacity, screen dimensions, color, sprite count, sound channels, controller inputs, storage, and communications are properties of a compatibility profile. Published programs state which optional facilities they require.</p>"},
      {id:"control",title:"Columns and procedures",html:"<p>In 学習三八, numbered columns are the standard branch targets. Named columns and procedures are manufacturer extensions. Conditions and loops supply ordinary structured repetition, while direct branches support the compact listings used by magazines and small machines.</p><p>学習四一 makes structured procedures part of the common language and supplies local parameters. Recursion is not required. This limit keeps interpreters practical on small systems and makes memory use easier to explain in introductory material.</p>"},
      {id:"graphics",title:"Graphics, sound, and controllers",html:"<p>Graphics, sound, and controllers are logical devices rather than fixed registers in the language. A standard command addresses a declared operation; the interpreter maps it to local hardware or reports that the facility is absent. Manufacturer profiles extend sprites, color, music, animation, and input devices.</p><p>学習四一 improves graphics and sound operations and adds common sprite commands. The language supports educational diagrams, demonstrations, household utilities, and games. Hardware-specific performance remains important, so commercial programs identify both the language generation and the compatible machine profile.</p>"},
      {id:"storage",title:"Storage and communications",html:"<p>学習三八 supports cassette storage and cartridge access. Cassettes carry editable source and data at low cost; cartridges can supply programs, libraries, or additional hardware. The standard does not require a game cartridge to expose its source.</p><p>学習四一 adds disk files, communications, stronger cartridge services, and common compatibility declarations. Programs can save larger source collections, exchange text, and use manufacturer or network adapters through documented profiles. File organization and communications protocols remain implementation-dependent.</p>"},
      {id:"g41",title:"学習四一",html:"<p>Introduced in 1981, 学習四一 revises the consumer language for a more capable generation of computers. It adds UNICODE 80 text, combined vertical and horizontal windows, improved graphics, sprite and sound commands, disk files, communications, cartridge access, structured procedures, and compatibility profiles across major manufacturers.</p><p>The language remains interpreted and accessible. Its standard procedures and local parameters allow larger programs without adopting the full module, type, and cross-compilation systems of professional languages. Existing 学習三八 programs can be supported through declared compatibility modes.</p>"},
      {id:"personal",title:"Sixteen-bit personal computers",html:"<p>A mature Japanese sixteen-bit personal-computer class appeared in 1982 with approximately 128 KB of starting memory, native Japanese display support, vertical programming mode, floppy storage, continuity with earlier peripherals, and optional or standard kanji ROM. Systems in this class supplied 学習四一 alongside professional-language support.</p><p>Gakushū remained the accessible environment for demonstrations, instruction, small utilities, and program exchange. Professional users could move to KEISAN, TEJUN, systems tools, or specialist languages on the same broader hardware ecosystem.</p>"},
      {id:"profiles",title:"Manufacturer compatibility profiles",html:"<p>Major firms share the educational core while extending graphics, music, controllers, storage, printers, communications, and cartridge hardware. A compatibility profile records the interpreter generation, character facilities, numeric options, screen and sound devices, storage interfaces, and extensions used by a program.</p><p>Magazines, clubs, schools, and publishers place the profile beside a listing or package. Some programs use only the national core; others require one manufacturer's machine family. Converters can replace tokens and device calls where two profiles provide comparable operations.</p>"},
      {id:"education",title:"Schools and technical clubs",html:"<p>Japanese children encounter programming through school clubs, home-computer magazines, programming contests, electronics stores, company-sponsored technical clubs, and television instruction. Lessons join arithmetic, conditions, repetition, coordinates, text, and logical problem-solving to native reading and vertical typography.</p><p>A learner does not need Latin characters to enter a complete program. Associated-state education can add local-script documentation, strings, comments, and keyboard aids while retaining Japanese control tokens and the common interpreter model.</p>"},
      {id:"games",title:"Games and program publication",html:"<p>Programming and games develop together. A finished cartridge offers controlled, tested software, while a cassette or printed magazine listing invites alteration. Users change graphics, rules, sound, and difficulty, then exchange source through clubs and publications.</p><p>Japan consequently supports both a polished console market and a participatory home-computer culture. Gakushū listings form part of the broader <a href='video-games-japan.html'>Japanese video-game industry</a>, educational publishing, and household software market.</p>"},
      {id:"localization",title:"Philippine and associated-state editions",html:"<p>Machines sold in the <a href='philippines.html'>Philippines</a> provide horizontal Latin mode, keyboard overlays, Filipino and English strings, optional Latin identifiers, Japanese control keywords, and sphere character support. Programs may combine Japanese grammar, Latin names, local-language comments, and Japanese or Arabic digits according to the customer profile.</p><p>Localization work is concentrated in Manila and Singapore. It includes keyboard and font support, translated manuals, examples, character conversion, peripheral profiles, and testing. Other associated-state editions use the same separation between common language tokens and locally displayed text.</p>"},
      {id:"distribution",title:"Software distribution",html:"<p>Gakushū software reaches users through hardware manufacturers, trading companies, licensed publishers, magazines, school systems, corporate dealerships, electronics retailers, and arcade distributors. Independent developers operate within this market, but national distribution commonly requires a corporate or publishing partner.</p><p>Distribution channels supply testing, documentation, customer support, and hardware integration. Listings and cassettes circulate more freely through clubs and periodicals. Cartridge publication is more tightly controlled because it depends on compatible hardware, manufacturing, and licensing.</p>"},
      {id:"feedback",title:"Consumer and industrial exchange",html:"<p>Consumer graphics chips, sound controllers, memory, processors, displays, and inexpensive peripherals also enter training simulators, operator consoles, machine tools, vehicle displays, portable test equipment, and communications systems. Industrial production and semiconductor investment reduce the price of household equipment.</p><p>Gakushū itself remains a consumer and educational language. The hardware and interface knowledge developed around its machines can move into professional equipment even when the resulting control software is written in TEJUN, KEISAN, or a machine-specific systems language.</p>"},
      {id:"position",title:"Position in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985, Gakushū is the normal first language on Japanese home systems and a common school environment. 学習四一 is the current standard, while 学習三八 compatibility remains useful for older machines, cassettes, magazines, and teaching material.</p><p>The family includes interpreters, compatibility profiles, token dictionaries, character mappings, manuals, publications, clubs, and a large body of educational, game, and household source. Professional programming remains divided among KEISAN, TEJUN, imported systems tools, and Japanese research languages.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"japanese-programming-languages.html",kicker:"National context",label:"Japanese Programming Languages"},{href:"keisan.html",kicker:"Scientific family",label:"KEISAN"},{href:"tejun.html",kicker:"Administrative and control family",label:"TEJUN"},{href:"unicode-80.html",kicker:"Universal text standard",label:"UNICODE 80"},{href:"co-prosperity-sphere-common-character-code.html",kicker:"Sphere character system",label:"Common Character Code"},{href:"video-games-japan.html",kicker:"Consumer software",label:"Video Games in Japan"},{href:"philippines.html",kicker:"Localized editions",label:"Philippine Republic"},{href:"software-portability.html",kicker:"Compatibility practice",label:"Software Portability"}],facts:[["First release","学習三八 (Gakushū 38), 1978"],["Current 1985 revision","学習四一 (Gakushū 41), 1981"],["Implementation","Interpreter"],["Source layout","Vertical, top-to-bottom; columns right-to-left"],["Integer model","16-bit"],["Floating point","Optional decimal"],["Strings","Length-counted"],["Arrays","One-origin"],["Workspace","Global variables"],["Storage","Cassette and cartridge; disk from 1981"],["Primary use","Home and education"]]
  }),

  "japanese-vertical-computing": softwareArticle({
    slug:"japanese-vertical-computing",title:"Vertical computing in Japan",eyebrow:"Japanese source and display practice · c. 1960–1985",infoboxKicker:"Computing and typography",
    lead:"<strong>Vertical computing in Japan</strong> is the family of source-writing, terminal, printing, and display practices that presents Japanese programs from the upper-right of a page, down through each statement, and then leftward through successive statement columns. It emerged from paper procedure sheets and native-script programming during the early 1960s, became the canonical view of KEISAN and TEJUN source, and later reached schools and homes through Gakushū and television displays. Horizontal regions remain part of the same environment for matrices, graphs, waveforms, imported text, and other material better read from left to right.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>",
    canon:"The upper-right, downward, and leftward reading order; vertical procedure sheets; serialized token and layout records; portrait 縦型端末 terminals from about 1962–64; page-buffered printing; kana and kanji token renderings; horizontal technical subwindows; Gakushū television layouts; 1982 Japanese personal-computer class; and the Common Character Code and UNICODE 80 relationship are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Exact terminal models, manufacturers, screen dimensions, control sequences, and institutional adoption shares remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origins",title:"Origins",html:"<p>Japanese programmers initially used numeric orders, symbolic addresses, Latin mathematical notation, Arabic numerals, punched cards, paper tape, and manufacturer-specific forms. At the same time, ministries and industrial firms described clerical and machine work on formal procedure sheets. The established Japanese page moved from the upper-right downward and then toward the left, while margins and frames separated annotations, authorities, inputs, and exception routes.</p><p>The growth of native-script KEISAN and TEJUN during the late 1950s made the paper convention a computing problem. A Japanese source form had to preserve statement order, nested structure, comments, and machine-readable identity without treating a page as an indivisible picture. The resulting practice joined Japanese typography to tokenized programming rather than translating an American horizontal editor.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"development",title:"Development",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Development</th><th>Typical medium</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Before 1960</td><td>Procedure sheets, cards, tape, numeric code, and mixed Latin, kana, and mathematical notation.</td><td>Portrait paper forms and machine listings.</td></tr><tr><td>1960–1964</td><td>Native vertical KEISAN and TEJUN source; early portrait 縦型端末 terminals appear.</td><td>Formal coding sheets, buffered printers, and specialist cathode-ray terminals.</td></tr><tr><td>1965–1970</td><td>Vertical terminals and common layout conventions become standard at major installations.</td><td>Multiple-column portrait displays and page-oriented editors.</td></tr><tr><td>1970–1977</td><td>Kanji rendering, mixed technical windows, and multilingual records become more capable.</td><td>Mainframe and industrial terminals alongside paper source books.</td></tr><tr><td>1978–1981</td><td>Gakushū carries vertical programming into homes; 学習四一 formalizes mixed windows.</td><td>Landscape household televisions and school computers.</td></tr><tr><td>1982–1985</td><td>Japanese sixteen-bit personal computers provide vertical modes, kanji ROMs, disks, and professional languages.</td><td>Personal computers, professional terminals, and networked workstations.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"coding-sheets",title:"Coding sheets",html:"<p>A formal programming sheet places the statement number at the top of a narrow column. The statement's characters and tokens descend below it. The following statement begins at the top of the next column to the left. Margin notes occupy a separate left-hand band, and block frames may span several statement columns to show nesting or a shared procedure.</p><p>This arrangement makes the sheet a structured document rather than prose written sideways. Reviewers can mark a column, follow a block frame, or add a diagnostic beside the affected statement. The upper-right-to-lower-left progression remains visible even when a program extends over several numbered pages.</p>"},
      {id:"serialization",title:"Serialized source",html:"<p>The machine-readable program is not stored as a rectangular array of glyphs. It is a logical stream of tokens, text, and layout records. Statement order follows the stream; the vertical page is its canonical editor view. Comments and string values retain their character identities, while layout records describe column boundaries, indentation, annotations, and mixed regions.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>This separation allows the same source to cross paper tape, punched cards, magnetic tape, disk files, and networks. It also permits different terminals to show the same token in katakana or an approved kanji form without changing the compiled program.</p>"},
      {id:"cards-tape",title:"Cards and paper tape",html:"<p>Card and tape equipment serializes one vertical column at a time. A statement number, its token sequence, and its layout controls are followed by the next statement to the left. Continuation and block records replace spatial relationships that a reader can see directly on the coding sheet.</p><p>Keypunch and verification staff therefore work from a visual page while producing an ordered stream. Conversion software can reconstruct a standardized page or emit a reduced horizontal listing for equipment without Japanese layout support.</p>"},
      {id:"printing",title:"Page-buffered printing",html:"<p>A line printer cannot normally place marks by following a vertical column. Japanese installations instead buffer a page in drum or core memory, calculate the position of every glyph, and print ordinary horizontal raster or impact rows that reconstruct the vertical columns on paper. Page composition precedes physical output.</p><p>Cheaper equipment may produce a horizontal transliteration or a token-number listing. Such output is useful for exchange and fault recovery but is regarded as a compatibility form rather than the authoritative Japanese source page.</p>"},
      {id:"terminals",title:"Vertical-form terminals",html:"<p>Purpose-built <strong>縦型端末</strong> (<em>tategata tanmatsu</em>, “vertical-form terminal”) appeared at leading installations around 1962–64. Its cathode-ray tube is mounted in portrait orientation. The cursor advances downward within the active statement and returns to the top of the next column on the left. By the later 1960s, major scientific, administrative, and industrial centers commonly used displays capable of showing several descending columns.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><p>The terminal is not defined by one processor or manufacturer. It is a device class implemented across incompatible corporate systems. Keyboard, font memory, local editing capacity, and communications speed vary, while cursor direction and page behavior remain recognizable.</p>"},
      {id:"editing",title:"Cursor and page editing",html:"<p>Early terminals edit one statement or a small group of columns at a time. Later page-oriented systems move among statement columns, insert or remove a column, shift a block frame, attach a margin annotation, and open a diagnostic beside the source. A selected token may be replaced without retyping its visible glyphs.</p><p>Program position is normally reported by page, statement, and token rather than by a horizontal line and character offset. This makes diagnostics stable when a richer terminal substitutes kanji for kana or displays a wider annotation column.</p>"},
      {id:"tokens",title:"Kana and kanji rendering",html:"<p>Reserved words are stored as language tokens. A compact terminal can display their katakana readings, while a terminal with a larger character generator can show an approved kanji form. The two renderings compile identically. This gives early machines a small repertoire without making their source a different language.</p><p>Identifiers, comments, and strings are more dependent on the installed character system. Numeric dictionary references and special printers preceded broad kanji display. Font memory, kanji ROMs, and registered character identities progressively made mixed Japanese source ordinary on professional equipment.</p>"},
      {id:"mixed-windows",title:"Horizontal technical windows",html:"<p>Vertical source does not require every object on a screen to be vertical. Editors open horizontal left-to-right regions for matrices, graphs, waveforms, engineering drawings, imported algorithms, Latin mathematical expressions, Philippine or English records, and tabular output. A window can be placed beside or below the program while the statement stream retains its vertical order.</p><p>This mixed arrangement reflects function rather than foreign prestige. A matrix is easier to compare by rows and columns, a waveform follows time across the screen, and an imported Latin record remains legible in its original direction. 学習四一 later carries the same principle into consumer machines.</p>"},
      {id:"languages",title:"Programming-language use",html:"<p><a href='keisan.html'>KEISAN</a> uses vertical source for scientific programs while opening horizontal areas for formulas, matrices, graphs, and instrument data. <a href='tejun.html'>TEJUN</a> uses statement columns and block bands for records, processes, states, devices, authority gates, and exception routes. The two families share presentation conventions without sharing one grammar or runtime.</p><p><a href='gakushu.html'>Gakushū</a> simplifies the model for interactive instruction. Numbered or named statement columns can be listed, altered, and executed directly. A learner can program entirely with Japanese tokens and numerals, although compatible editions may add Latin identifiers or localized text.</p>"},
      {id:"paper",title:"Paper and terminal coexistence",html:"<p>Portrait terminals do not immediately displace paper. Large programs circulate as bound vertical source books containing revision marks, block diagrams, record layouts, device lists, test results, and operator notes. Paper supports desk review, signatures, change control, and consultation beside machinery where a terminal is unavailable.</p><p>Programmers often prepare or annotate a sheet before keying the revision. Installation libraries retain both the serialized source and an accepted page image or printout. The coexistence of paper and display helps stabilize conventions across successive generations of hardware.</p>"},
      {id:"home",title:"Household television displays",html:"<p>From the late 1970s, household computers adapt vertical source to ordinary landscape television sets. The earliest statement occupies the rightmost column, characters descend, and later statements appear to the left. A horizontal strip along the bottom can report immediate results, status, storage messages, or device output.</p><p>The screen shows fewer and shorter columns than a professional portrait terminal. Tokenization reduces memory use and input time, while scrolling or paging exposes the remainder of the program. Home users therefore learn the same reading order without buying specialized display hardware.</p>"},
      {id:"personal-computers",title:"Personal computers after 1982",html:"<p>A Japanese sixteen-bit personal-computer class emerged in 1982 with about 128 KB of starting memory, floppy storage, continuity with older peripherals, native Japanese display, a vertical programming mode, and optional or standard kanji ROM. These systems could provide Gakushū beside professional KEISAN, TEJUN, and systems-language tools.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><p>Professional portrait terminals remained preferable for long source pages, but personal machines widened access to mixed-window editing, disk libraries, communications, and high-quality Japanese output. Orientation became an operating mode as well as a physical property of the screen.</p>"},
      {id:"characters",title:"Character standards",html:"<p>The <a href='imperial-kana-machine-code.html'>Imperial Kana Machine Code</a> supplied compact katakana, Japanese numerals, punctuation, controls, and vertical-layout instructions during the late 1950s. The <a href='co-prosperity-sphere-common-character-code.html'>Co-Prosperity Sphere Common Character Code</a> added registered identities and vertical or horizontal rendering properties from 1962–63.</p><p><a href='unicode-80.html'>UNICODE 80</a> preserved abstract character identity while supporting left-to-right, right-to-left, and top-to-bottom presentation. Japanese columns progress from right to left, punctuation receives vertical forms, and combining marks remain attached to the logical character sequence. Local compact encodings can continue behind a conforming interchange layer.</p>"},
      {id:"multilingual",title:"Multilingual use",html:"<p>Vertical Japanese programs frequently process horizontal or differently directed records. Philippine installations use Latin-script names, Filipino and English text, and imported American formats beside Japanese control tokens. Chinese, Korean, Thai, Burmese, and other sphere records require their own character and collation profiles.</p><p>Editors assign writing direction to a region or string rather than to the entire machine. Gateways translate local encodings and preserve registered identities where possible. Japanese-centered systems can display multilingual records without forcing their contents into vertical kana.</p>"},
      {id:"hardware",title:"Hardware and industry",html:"<p>Vertical computing stimulated demand for portrait cathode-ray assemblies, addressable displays, font memory, kanji generators, bitmap graphics, page buffers, character printers, keyboard conversion, and electronic typesetting. Corporate mainframes, minicomputers, industrial stations, and consumer electronics shared parts and manufacturing knowledge even when their software was incompatible.</p><p>The feedback operated in both directions. Industrial investment improved displays and semiconductor production; mass-market televisions, memories, and character components lowered the cost of operator stations and technical education. Japanese vertical support consequently became an ecosystem capability rather than a feature of one computer family.</p>"},
      {id:"work",title:"Professional practice",html:"<p>Training includes the reading order of source pages, statement and block navigation, token input, character conversion, mixed-window use, and recovery from a reduced listing. Reviewers distinguish the logical program from a particular font or page rendering. Operators learn which windows show executable source, live process state, imported data, or diagnostic annotation.</p><p>This discipline is especially important in factories and administrative systems, where source accompanies procedure diagrams, authorities, device maps, and restart instructions. The page makes those relationships visible but does not replace compiler tests, machine profiles, or operational documentation.</p>"},
      {id:"position",title:"Position in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985, vertical programming is normal in Japanese native-language computing. KEISAN and TEJUN retain the professional portrait-page model; Gakushū gives schools and homes a compact television version; personal computers combine both orientations; and UNICODE 80 carries the underlying character identities across networks and machines.</p><p>The system is neither a rotated American terminal nor an exclusively vertical desktop. Its defining feature is a logical source stream whose canonical Japanese view descends from the upper-right, with horizontal and multilingual regions opened wherever the represented material requires them.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"japanese-programming-languages.html",kicker:"Software context",label:"Japanese Programming Languages"},{href:"keisan.html",kicker:"Scientific source",label:"KEISAN"},{href:"tejun.html",kicker:"Procedural source",label:"TEJUN"},{href:"gakushu.html",kicker:"Home and educational use",label:"Gakushū"},{href:"imperial-kana-machine-code.html",kicker:"Early character system",label:"Imperial Kana Machine Code"},{href:"co-prosperity-sphere-common-character-code.html",kicker:"Sphere text system",label:"Common Character Code"},{href:"unicode-80.html",kicker:"Universal character identity",label:"UNICODE 80"},{href:"philippines.html",kicker:"Multilingual environment",label:"Philippine Republic"},{href:"computing.html",kicker:"Technology overview",label:"Computing"},{href:"software-portability.html",kicker:"Exchange and compatibility",label:"Software Portability"}],facts:[["Canonical direction","Upper-right, downward, then leftward <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>"],["Japanese term","縦型端末"],["First vertical-form terminals","About 1962–64"],["Standard professional display","Portrait cathode-ray terminal"],["Stored representation","Logical token stream with layout records <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Keyword display","Katakana or approved kanji from the same token"],["Horizontal regions","Matrices, graphs, waveforms, imported and Latin text"],["Home implementation","Landscape television with vertical columns"],["Personal-computer class","Japanese sixteen-bit systems from 1982"],["Current character standard","UNICODE 80"],["Professional languages","KEISAN and TEJUN"],["Educational language","Gakushū"]]
  }),

  "latin-programming-languages": softwareArticle({
    slug:"latin-programming-languages",title:"Latin-bloc programming languages",eyebrow:"Latin Catholic bloc · 1947–1985",infoboxKicker:"Multinational software culture",
    lead:"Programming in the Latin bloc developed around the computer as an intelligible instrument. Italian office and industrial systems, Occitan aerospace and scientific work, Spanish education and manufacturing, Portuguese communications, multilingual standards, and common technical institutions produced strong ALGOL, LIA, real-time, Pascal, Modula, and Prolog traditions. No ministry or company imposed one compulsory bloc language. Programs moved through documented source profiles, record schemas, libraries, conversion tools, and negotiated interfaces.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>",
    canon:"The Italian and Occitan origins, ALGOL-Latin 62 and 68, the LIA generations, plural Occitan control languages, Marseille Prolog, imported Pascal, Modula, C, BASIC and Ada lineages, multilingual interoperability, and the absence of a single bloc language are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> LIA's final commercial expansion, the exact editions selected by individual institutions, bit assignments for the Latin character code, precise market shares, and names of individual Occitan operational-control languages remain open. A single invented umbrella control language would contradict the established plurality.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origins",title:"Machine and symbolic beginnings",html:"<p>Italian programming after 1947 grew from machine orders, symbolic addresses, punched-card workflows, decimal routines, report generators, printers, magnetic tape, and analysis of office procedures. <a href='olivetti.html'>Olivetti</a> treated prompts, correction, readable errors, audit trails, forms, and modular applications as parts of the installed system. Business customers purchased equipment together with forms, training, maintenance, and procedures.</p><p>Occitan work grew from formula translation, numerical libraries, radar, instruments, assembly, real-time sequences, and reusable aerospace routines. Spanish schools combined analog and relay instruction with imported Italian office practice and Occitan scientific work. Portugal's port, merchant, telecommunications, and colonial connections supplied communications and data-exchange requirements. No general Latin high-level language existed before ALGOL.</p>"},
      {id:"chronology",title:"Development, 1947–1985",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Principal language development</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1947–1955</td><td>Italian office-machine and record programming; Occitan symbolic, numerical, instrument, and control routines; Spanish analog and relay instruction.</td></tr><tr><td>1955–1960</td><td>ALGOL 58/60 becomes the neutral scientific base. Olivetti develops LIA-59; batch monitors, printers, tape, transistors, and magnetic core enlarge practical use.</td></tr><tr><td>1960–1965</td><td>ALGOL-Latin 62 standardizes scientific services. LIA-63 consolidates administrative records and exact decimal work.</td></tr><tr><td>1965–1970</td><td>ALGOL-Latin 68 adds modules, records, simulation, limited events, and devices. LIA 67/69 adds terminals, indexed data, logging, restart, and Scudo libraries. Marseille logic research begins.</td></tr><tr><td>1970–1975</td><td>Marseille Prolog emerges around 1972. LIA 73 adds online forms and transactions. Pascal, Modula, databases, workstations, and microprocessors enter the professional field.</td></tr><tr><td>1975–1980</td><td>Consumer Pascal and BASIC interpreters accompany Olivetti-centered home and professional systems. Prolog becomes an international research language.</td></tr><tr><td>1980–1985</td><td>UNICODE 80, industrial Prolog, Ada, C, Modula-family systems, public networks, graphical desktops, and joint spaceflight expand the multilingual software commons.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"interchange",title:"Interchange before common languages",html:"<p>The first shared standards described media labels, decimal records, and peripheral character mappings rather than machine instructions. Italian decimal systems and Occitan binary machines exchanged explicit schemas and conversion programs. A record description identified field names, lengths, signs, decimal scale, character mapping, validation, and rejected-data treatment.</p><p>This layer did not imply common arithmetic or object code. It allowed customs records, payrolls, shipping documents, experimental measurements, and industrial orders to travel among systems that calculated differently. The practice remained important after higher-level languages appeared because local operating systems and peripheral formats continued to differ.</p>"},
      {id:"algol",title:"ALGOL-Latin",html:"<p>Italy and Occitania adopted ALGOL 58 and ALGOL 60 as a politically neutral scientific language. Mathematical notation and the international report avoided choosing Italian, French, Spanish, Portuguese, or Occitan as the bloc's programming vocabulary. Universities and laboratories then standardized the practical services missing from the publication language.</p><p><a href='algol-latin.html'>ALGOL-Latin 62</a> added input and output, complex values, matrices, declared precision, named errors, tape and file services, and separately compiled procedures. ALGOL-Latin 68 added records, modules, simulation packages, limited event and parallel facilities, and explicit assembly and device linkage. Universities, nuclear engineering, navigation, aerospace, orbital calculation, construction, and advanced manufacturing used the family.</p><p>Programs declared the edition, numeric range, precision, file profile, matrix library, and device modules they required. MODELO and MODÈLE simulation packages reproduced selected class, process, queue, and event ideas without requiring a German compiler or becoming a separate language.</p>"},
      {id:"lia",title:"LIA administrative family",html:"<p><a href='lia-language.html'>LIA</a> began as Olivetti's compact administrative family for records, exact decimal money, magnetic tape, sorting, reports, payroll, inventory, customs, shipping, and banking. LIA-59 and LIA-63 served batch work. LIA 67/69 added terminals, indexed records, logging, restart, recovery, and Latin Scudo libraries. LIA 73 added screen forms, online transactions, report generators, and packages for settlement, Suez traffic, and shipping.</p><p>Decimal fields declared precision and scale. Binary floating point could enter a money field only through an explicit conversion and rounding rule. Fixed-layout records, sequential or indexed files, journals, checkpoints, operator stations, reject files, and audit output made failure and recovery visible. LIA supplied an administrative counterpart to ALGOL-Latin science.</p>"},
      {id:"control",title:"Occitan real-time languages",html:"<p>Occitan radar, missile, avionics, naval, artillery, telemetry, and industrial projects used assembly, ALGOL-derived engineering languages, and compact project control languages. Channels, events, tracks, timers, devices, interrupts, states, deadlines, and named failure routes recurred across systems.</p><p>Projects commonly tied a compiler, runtime, device map, test set, and hardware configuration to one accepted operational release. Language families moved between related applications, but operational software remained more tightly bound to equipment and certification than scientific source. No single bloc-wide control language existed by 1985, and the individual operational languages remain unnamed in the controlling reference.</p>"},
      {id:"prolog",title:"Marseille Prolog",html:"<p><a href='marseille-prolog.html'>Marseille Prolog</a> emerged in Occitania around 1972 from language analysis, symbolic reasoning, and theorem-proving work. Programs stated facts, relations, Horn-clause rules, and goals. Unification and ordered depth-first backtracking searched for answers. Lists supplied the principal recursive structure; files, instruments, terminals, and databases entered through documented external predicates.</p><p>Early uses included translation, law, symbolic mathematics, proof, planning, diagnosis, and technical knowledge. By 1985 experimental and commercial systems appeared in maintenance, customs, medicine, military planning, and expert systems. Britain contributed theory and implementation. Japan became the largest foreign investor, Germany developed its own Prolog-influenced Schluss family, and American universities used the language in artificial-intelligence research.</p>"},
      {id:"imported",title:"Imported and shared languages",html:"<p>The bloc normally preserved an imported language's international name. <a href='pascal-programming-language.html'>Pascal</a> became the principal structured teaching language. <a href='modula-programming-language.html'>Modula</a>-family systems brought explicit interfaces, separately compiled modules, abstract data, and controlled access to devices. C served operating systems, instruments, networks, and professional microcomputers. BASIC and Pascal interpreters served household machines.</p><p><a href='ada-programming-language.html'>Ada</a> entered multinational military and aerospace work under an American-led standard. Its packages, strong types, representation clauses, tasks, and exceptions suited joint procurement when projects fixed their compiler, machine, and certified subset. FORTRAN, assembly, COBOL, and other international languages also survived where institutions retained libraries and trained staff.</p>"},
      {id:"text",title:"Text and terminology",html:"<p>Italian, Spanish, Portuguese, French, and Occitan source, documentation, records, and operator messages required accented characters and agreed technical vocabulary. Early machines used a Latin eight-bit interchange code and machine-specific mappings. Programs distinguished the abstract character, the displayed glyph, the external record form, and the local byte representation.</p><p><a href='unicode-80.html'>UNICODE 80</a> supplied common sixteen-bit character identities after 1980. The Latin bloc enforced the standard early and used it for international records, Prolog atoms, public systems, and technical exchange. Local compact encodings, fonts, keyboard layouts, collations, operating systems, and file formats remained distinct.</p>"},
      {id:"division",title:"National division of work",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Member</th><th>Principal software and institutional strengths</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href='italy.html'>Italy</a></td><td>Olivetti office and desktop systems, LIA, business applications, banking, Scudo settlement, public corporations, industrial design, and system maintenance.</td></tr><tr><td><a href='occitania.html'>Occitania</a></td><td>Scientific calculation, aerospace, radar, avionics, telemetry, real-time control, Marseille Prolog, expert systems, and electronics integration.</td></tr><tr><td><a href='spain.html'>Spain</a></td><td>Technical education, manufacturing, licensed implementations, military and aerospace software, and common-language institutional adoption.</td></tr><tr><td><a href='portugal.html'>Portugal</a></td><td>Ports, shipping records, telecommunications, Atlantic and imperial links, tracking, gateway services, and distributed operational support.</td></tr><tr><td>Joint institutions</td><td>ALGOL-Latin editions, character standards, administrative terminology, Scudo libraries, network gateways, procurement interfaces, and spaceflight certification.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>National operating systems, manufacturers, and project languages continued to compete. Common standards specified what had to cross an organizational boundary rather than transferring all authority to Rome or Toulouse.</p>"},
      {id:"education",title:"Education and professions",html:"<p>Students commonly met Pascal before specialist work. Scientific and engineering courses used ALGOL-Latin and later Modula-family tools. Administrative programmers learned LIA record, decimal, audit, and recovery practice. Aerospace and control engineers learned the local language and hardware profile of an employer or project. Prolog entered advanced language, logic, diagnosis, and planning courses.</p><p>Software teams included systems analysts, numerical programmers, device engineers, operators, librarians, test staff, technical translators, and maintenance personnel. Complete professional installations required forms, manuals, terminology, input rules, recovery exercises, and trained operators in addition to executable programs.</p>"},
      {id:"portability",title:"Portability and certification",html:"<p>Source exchange depended on declared numeric precision, character mapping, file schema, compiler edition, libraries, operating services, device modules, and target hardware. Scientific programs often moved at source level. LIA moved records and procedures through defined administrative profiles. Certified real-time software required new timing, hardware, and failure tests on each operational target.</p><p>The bloc's interface practice joined multilingual record standards to versioned modules and documented conversion. It did not promise one binary for every machine. See <a href='software-portability.html'>software portability</a> for the distinction among algorithmic, source-family, source-profile, intermediate, and binary compatibility.</p>"},
      {id:"applications",title:"Networks and joint programs",html:"<p>The <a href='latin-technical-network.html'>Latin Technical Network</a> grew from Olivetti business systems, Italian public corporations, Scudo clearing, ports, Occitan scientific computing, Iberian telecommunications, and public administrative services. Gateways exchanged selected messages with other blocs while national and institutional systems retained their own files and applications.</p><p>Joint spaceflight required Italian launch systems, Occitan avionics, Spanish manufacture, Portuguese tracking, shared records, and common operational terminology. <a href='aquila-i.html'>Aquila I</a> in 1984 demonstrated that the four principal members could certify and operate a common mission without adopting a German, American, or Japanese master software architecture.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"identity",title:"Position in 1985",html:"<p>Italy led professional desktop design and administrative systems. Occitania led industrial logic programming and specialized control. ALGOL-Latin remained the prestigious scientific archive language. LIA organized commercial records, while Pascal dominated education. Modula and C expanded in systems work. Ada entered protected aerospace and military programs. Prolog connected Latin research to a worldwide logic-programming community.</p><p>The bloc's shared software culture consisted of several national industries and international language lineages joined by common interfaces, text standards, documentation, and institutions. It possessed no compulsory language, common processor, or universal operating system.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"}
    ],related:[...latinSoftwareRelated,{href:"algol-latin.html",kicker:"Scientific common language",label:"ALGOL-Latin"},{href:"olivetti.html",kicker:"Commercial systems center",label:"Olivetti"},{href:"unicode-80.html",kicker:"Multilingual identity",label:"UNICODE 80"},{href:"latin-technical-network.html",kicker:"Network environment",label:"Latin Technical Network"},{href:"software-portability.html",kicker:"Comparative framework",label:"Software Portability"}],facts:[["Governing conception","Computer as intelligible instrument <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>"],["First shared scientific edition","ALGOL-Latin 62"],["Second scientific edition","ALGOL-Latin 68"],["Administrative family","LIA, from 1959"],["Logic-programming origin","Marseille Prolog, c. 1972"],["Principal teaching language","Pascal"],["Real-time center","Occitania"],["Commercial-systems center","Italy and Olivetti"],["Shared text","Latin eight-bit; UNICODE 80 after 1980"],["Common program exchange","Source profiles, records, modules, and interfaces"],["Single bloc language","None <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>"],["Unnamed field","Individual Occitan control languages"]]
  }),

  "lia-language": softwareArticle({
    slug:"lia-language",title:"LIA (programming language)",eyebrow:"Italian administrative language family · 1959–1985",infoboxKicker:"Olivetti administrative system",
    lead:"LIA, originally expanded as <em>Linguaggio Italiano Amministrativo</em>, is Olivetti's compact administrative programming-language family for exact money, records, files, payroll, inventory, customs, shipping, banking, terminals, and settlement systems.",
    canon:"The 1959/63, 1967/69, and 1973 generations, Olivetti origin, decimal and record model, audit, recovery, and Latin Scudo role are established. Whether LIA remains the final commercial expansion of the name is open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origin",title:"LIA-59 and LIA-63",html:"<p>The first generations turn Italian office-procedure analysis into a compact language of records, exact decimals, tapes, sorting, reports, payroll, inventory, customs, shipping, and banking.</p><p>Block-form declarations describe data before concise imperative procedures transform it.</p>"},
      {id:"decimal",title:"Money and decimal rules",html:"<p>Decimal fields declare precision and scale. Binary floating point cannot enter a money field without an explicit conversion and rounding rule.</p><p>This makes numerical responsibility visible in source and prevents platform-dependent approximation from silently changing accounts.</p>"},
      {id:"records",title:"Records and files",html:"<p>Records have fixed layouts with named overlays. Files are sequential, indexed, or transaction journals. Sorting, reports, validated fields, and reject routes are standard administrative operations rather than external conventions.</p>"},
      {id:"online",title:"LIA 67 and 69",html:"<p>The late-1960s generations add terminals, indexed data, logging, restart, recovery, and libraries for the Latin Scudo. Batch and online work coexist as banks, customs offices, and transport companies connect their records.</p>"},
      {id:"lia73",title:"LIA 73",html:"<p>The 1973 generation adds screen forms, online transactions, report generators, and packages for settlement, Suez traffic, and shipping. Form fields bind validated input directly to record definitions.</p>"},
      {id:"transactions",title:"Journal and checkpoint model",html:"<p>A transaction groups record changes with its audit output. Early implementations use a write-ahead journal and checkpoint rather than promising general concurrent isolation.</p><p>Errors route to a handler, an operator station, or a reject file; recovery restores the last committed administrative state.</p>"},
      {id:"bloc",title:"Role in the Latin bloc",html:"<p>LIA provides the administrative counterpart to ALGOL-Latin science. Its libraries help national currencies, the Latin Scudo, ports, customs systems, and firms exchange records while preserving explicit conversion and audit.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy and naming",html:"<p>By 1985 LIA is a mature family embedded in long-lived institutional systems. Its influence lies in readable errors, controlled decimals, operator-visible recovery, and professional desktop applications.</p><p>The letters LIA are fixed in use, though archival debate continues over whether the original expansion remained the final commercial name.</p>"}
    ],related:latinSoftwareRelated,facts:[["Developer","Olivetti-centered Italian industry"],["First generations","LIA-59 and LIA-63"],["Online generations","LIA 67/69 and LIA 73"],["Primary data","Records and exact decimal fields"],["Recovery","Write-ahead journal and checkpoint"],["Monetary library","Latin Scudo"],["Primary fields","Payroll, customs, shipping, banking"],["Name status","LIA fixed; expansion debated"]]
  }),

  "marseille-prolog": softwareArticle({
    slug:"marseille-prolog",title:"Marseille Prolog",eyebrow:"Occitan logic programming · from 1972",infoboxKicker:"Logic-programming language family",
    lead:"Marseille Prolog is the Occitan-origin logic-programming language developed around 1972. Programs state facts, relations, and rules; users pose goals that the system attempts to satisfy through unification and ordered search. By 1985 it serves research and selected institutional expert systems across several blocs.",
    canon:"The Marseille origin, approximate 1972 date, core search model, early uses, British cooperation, Japanese investment, and international position are established. Later standard details vary by implementation.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origin",title:"Origin at Marseille",html:"<p>Work in language analysis, symbolic reasoning, and theorem proving produces a practical relational language at Marseille around 1972. Occitania retains the accepted origin claim, while British logicians and implementers contribute theory and tools.</p>"},
      {id:"model",title:"Facts, rules, and goals",html:"<p>A program consists of first-order terms, facts, Horn-clause rules, and goals. Logical variables are bound by unification as the system attempts to prove the requested relation.</p><p>Lists provide the principal recursive data structure.</p>"},
      {id:"search",title:"Search behavior",html:"<p>Goals are tried left to right, clauses top to bottom, using depth-first backtracking. A cut operation commits to selected choices; explicit failure forces another branch.</p><p>The practical engine omits the occurs-check, gaining speed at the cost of permitting cyclic or nonlogical edge cases.</p>"},
      {id:"effects",title:"Arithmetic and external effects",html:"<p>Arithmetic uses explicit evaluation predicates rather than ordinary logical equality. Files, terminals, instruments, and databases appear through extralogical predicates.</p><p>Their side effects are not undone by backtracking unless an implementation documents a special transactional service.</p>"},
      {id:"modules",title:"Modules and text interchange",html:"<p>After 1980, the exchange form uses UNICODE-80 text so atoms preserve accents and script identity. A module declares exported predicates by name and arity.</p><p>Implementations expose depth and heap limits and permit an operator to interrupt a search that would otherwise exhaust institutional resources.</p>"},
      {id:"uses",title:"Early and later uses",html:"<p>Early applications include language analysis, translation, law, symbolic mathematics, proof, planning, and diagnosis. By 1985 experimental and commercial systems appear in maintenance, customs, medicine, military planning, and technical knowledge bases.</p>"},
      {id:"international",title:"International development",html:"<p>Britain contributes implementation and formal work; Germany's Schluss adapts the paradigm; Japan becomes the largest foreign investor and develops Ronri 44; American universities incorporate Prolog into artificial-intelligence research.</p><p>International spread does not erase Marseille's historical priority.</p>"},
      {id:"limits",title:"Limits and legacy",html:"<p>Prolog can express institutional knowledge compactly, but its answer depends on clause order, search strategy, resource limits, and the accuracy of facts. It does not make policy neutral or convert every administrative problem into logic.</p><p>Its enduring achievement is to make relations and inference, rather than a fixed sequence of commands, the central unit of programming.</p>"}
    ],related:latinSoftwareRelated,facts:[["Origin","Marseille, Occitania"],["First practical work","Around 1972"],["Paradigm","Logic programming"],["Core mechanism","Unification and backtracking"],["Search","Depth-first, ordered"],["Occurs-check","Omitted in practical engine"],["Largest foreign investor","Japan"],["1985 status","Research and selected commercial use"]]
  })
});
